<?xml version="1.0"?>
<rdf:RDF xmlns="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#"
     xml:base="http://purl.org/ontoturnover"
     xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"
     xmlns:dct="http://purl.org/dc/terms/"
     xmlns:owl="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#"
     xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"
     xmlns:xml="http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace"
     xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#"
     xmlns:foaf="http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/"
     xmlns:gufo="http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#"
     xmlns:rdfs="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#"
     xmlns:time="http://www.w3.org/2006/time#"
     xmlns:vann="http://purl.org/vocab/vann/">
    <owl:Ontology rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover">
        <owl:versionIRI rdf:resource="http://purl.org/ontoturnover"/>
        <owl:imports rdf:resource="http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#/1.0.0"/>
        <dc:creator>Santana, Aline</dc:creator>
        <dc:title xml:lang="en">OntoTurnover: A Comprehensive gUFO Ontology for Understanding and Modeling Employee Turnover</dc:title>
        <dct:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">SANTANA, A. C.; RODRIGUES, C. M. d. O.; FARIAS JUNIOR, I. H. d.; SANTOS, W. B. OntoTurnover: A Lightweight Domain Ontology for Modeling Employee Turnover. 2023 18th Iberian Conference on Information Systems and Technologies (CISTI), Aveiro, Portugal, 2023, pp. 1-4, doi: 10.23919/CISTI58278.2023.10211799. https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10211799

SANTANA, Aline C.; FARIAS JUNIOR, Ivaldir H. de; SANTOS, Wylliams B.; RODRIGUES, Cleyton M. de O.. OntoTurnover: Uma ontologia de domínio sobre a rotatividade de profissionais. In: WORKSHOP DE TESES E DISSERTAÇÕES EM QUALIDADE DE SOFTWARE - SIMPÓSIO BRASILEIRO DE QUALIDADE DE SOFTWARE (SBQS), 22. , 2023, Brasília/DF. Anais [...]. Porto Alegre: Sociedade Brasileira de Computação, 2023 . p. 13-18. DOI: https://doi.org/10.5753/sbqs_estendido.2023.235887.</dct:bibliographicCitation>
        <dct:created xml:lang="en">2026-08-20</dct:created>
        <dct:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode"/>
        <dct:modified xml:lang="en">2026-09-20</dct:modified>
        <vann:preferredNamespacePrefix xml:lang="en">OntoTurnover</vann:preferredNamespacePrefix>
        <vann:preferredNamespaceUri>http://purl.org/ontoturnover</vann:preferredNamespaceUri>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">OntoTurnover is a core domain ontology designed to explicitly, formally, and visually represent the complex phenomena of employee turnover (both voluntary and involuntary) and professional retention.

Originally conceived under the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO), OntoTurnover has been structurally re-engineered into gUFO (Gentle UFO), a lightweight implementation optimized for OWL 2 DL and Semantic Web knowledge graphs. Under this gUFO baseline, the ontology maps the domain through precise metaphysical categories:
1. Physical Agents and Roles: Human individuals (gufo:Kind) assume the relational, anti-rigid role of an Employee (gufo:Role), which is mediated by an Employment relationship (gufo:Relator).
2. Intrinsic Aspects: Personal states of well-being, such as Burnout, Job Satisfaction, and Psychological Capital, are treated as intrinsic aspects (gufo:IntrinsicAspect) or mental modes (gufo:IntrinsicMode) inhering in the employee.
3. Perdurants (Events and Situations): Turnover and Retention are modeled as events (gufo:Event) that trigger transitions between a pre-state situation (causes) and a post-state situation (consequences).

Core Objectives
1. Unification of Fragmented Literature: To consolidate over a century of interdisciplinary, fragmented, and sometimes contradictory scientific research on turnover and retention into a single, unified conceptual model.
2. Semantic Rectification: To resolve critical conceptual anomalies in HR literature and existing IT models—such as correcting structural errors where employees were modeled as subclasses of institutional/organizational agents.
3. Formalization of Cause-and-Effect Chains: To establish clear, axiomatized logical pathways connecting workplace situations (causes) to mental states (intentions), actual actions (turnover/retention), and organizational or individual impacts (consequences).
4. Automated Reasoning: To implement logical rules and constraints that enable computational reasoners to perform automated inference, diagnosing turnover risks in real employee profiles.

Possible Applications
- Smart HR &amp; Talent Management Integration: Integrating the ontology with corporate Personnel Administration (payroll, benefits) and Talent Management systems (climate surveys, performance reviews) to automatically run inference checks and identify employees at risk of leaving.
- Reducing Generative AI Hallucinations in HR: Using OntoTurnover&apos;s strict semantic rules, relations, and constraints as a grounded knowledge graph to enrich corporate AI tools, thereby improving decision-making accuracy and reducing semantic errors.
- Strategic Risk Mapping &amp; Policymaking: Helping organization leaders visually map systemic risks (such as how salary dissatisfaction or poor leadership relationships translate to turnover intentions) and design targeted retention strategies.
- A Foundation for Academic Research: Providing a highly structured semantic baseline that researchers can reuse and extend to develop specialized, domain-specific ontologies (e.g., studying turnover exclusively in Software Development, Healthcare, or under remote work transitions).</rdfs:comment>
        <owl:versionInfo xml:lang="en">1.0.0</owl:versionInfo>
    </owl:Ontology>
    


