    ///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
     -->

    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Action">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Event type that classifies intentional, goal-directed happenings performed by agents.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, Action is the common supertype of DecisionMaking (deliberation) and DecisionResultingAction (turnover and retention outcomes). DecisionMaking and DecisionResultingAction are disjoint.

Examples include an employee deliberating about an offer, or a company carrying out a dismissal.

Synonyms: intentional action; agentive action; intentional event; deliberate act; purposeful act; goal-directed action.

References:

Guizzardi, G., Benevides, A. B., Fonseca, C. M., Porello, D., Almeida, J. P. A., &amp; Sales, T. P. (2022). UFO: Unified Foundational Ontology. Applied Ontology, 17(1), 167–210. https://doi.org/10.3233/AO-210256

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ActiveOrganization">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Phase (anti-rigid sortal) of JuridicalPerson representing the operational, legally active status of an organization.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, an organization must be active to establish new employment relationships; the phase is disjoint from ExtinctOrganization.

Examples include a company that is operating and hiring employees.

Synonyms: operating organization; functioning organization; extant organization; ongoing organization; active enterprise.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Adolescent">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Phase (anti-rigid sortal) of LivingPerson representing the transitional stage between childhood and legal adulthood.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, adolescents may play the Employee role in legally protected arrangements, such as apprenticeships.

Examples include a young apprentice or intern entering the workforce.

Synonyms: teenager; teen; young person; youth; juvenile.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Adult">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Phase (anti-rigid sortal) of LivingPerson representing a human being with full biological, cognitive, and legal maturity.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, adults form the main population playing the Employee role.

Examples include a 35-year-old analyst.

Synonyms: grown person; mature person; person of legal age; adult individual.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Affiliation">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s subjective sense of belonging, connection, and social attachment to their team or organization.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an analyst who describes the team as &quot;my people&quot; and declines outside offers because of it.

Synonyms: belongingness; sense of belonging; social connectedness; organizational identification; group attachment; social affiliation.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Agent">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Category An abstract class that classifies entities with intentional and causal capabilities, enabling them to perform actions, pursue goals, and hold mental states.

OntoTurnover/gUFO application: Agent specializes gufo:Object at the individual level and is typed rdf:type gufo:Category at the class level. This distinction allows people and institutional agents to retain individual-level identity and participation in events without treating the agents themselves as types.

Synonyms: intentional agent; acting entity; intentional entity; actor; agentive entity

References:
- Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A lightweight implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#AlignmentBetweenRecognitionReceived">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s perception that the recognition and rewards received are congruent with their effort and contribution.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an engineer who feels a recent promotion fairly reflects the results delivered.

Synonyms: Effort-Reward Congruence; Fair Recognition; Alignment Between Acknowledgment Received; Alignment Between Appreciation Received; Alignment Between Employee Recognition Received.

References:

Ho, S., Stenhouse, R., &amp; Snowden, A. (2021). ‘It was quite a shock’: A qualitative study of the impact of organisational and personal factors on newly qualified nurses&apos; experiences. Journal of Clinical Nursing. https://doi.org/10.1111/jocn.15777

Hirschi, A., &amp; Spurk, D. (2021). Ambitious employees: Why and when ambition relates to performance and organizational commitment. Journal of Vocational Behavior. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jvb.2021.103576

Chênevert, D., Hill, K., &amp; Kilroy, S. (2022). Employees perceptions of non‐monetary recognition practice and turnover: Does recognition source alignment and contrast matter? Human Resource Management Journal. https://doi.org/10.1111/1748-8583.12354

Farooq, H., Janjua, U. I., Madni, T. M., Waheed, A., Zareei, M., &amp; Alanazi, F. (2022). Identification and Analysis of Factors Influencing Turnover Intention of Pakistan IT Professionals: An Empirical Study. IEEE Access. https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2022.3181753

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#AlignmentWithTheEmployersPurpose">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s subjective alignment with, and commitment to, the mission, values, and societal purpose of the employing organization.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a public health researcher who stays because they identify with the institute&apos;s mission.

Synonyms: organizational purpose alignment; purpose congruence; mission alignment; employee–organization purpose fit; goal congruence; shared purpose; Purpose Alignment.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Autonomy">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a work arrangement in which employees have substantial freedom to decide how, when, and in what order to perform their work.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a designer who defines their own methods and priorities for each project.

Synonyms: job autonomy; work autonomy; workplace autonomy; job control; decision latitude; self-determination at work; task discretion; Job Self-Determination; Workplace Autonomy in job design theories; Self-Determination.

References:

Asakura, K., Asakura, T., Satoh, M., Watanabe, I., &amp; Hara, Y. (2020). Health indicators as moderators of occupational commitment and nurses&apos; intention to leave. Japan Journal of Nursing Science. https://doi.org/10.1111/jjns.12277

Farooq, H., Janjua, U. I., Madni, T. M., Waheed, A., Zareei, M., &amp; Alanazi, F. (2022). Identification and Analysis of Factors Influencing Turnover Intention of Pakistan IT Professionals: An Empirical Study. IEEE Access. https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2022.3181753

Astvik, W., Welander, JO., &amp; Larsson, R. (2020). Reasons for staying: A longitudinal study of work conditions predicting social workers’ willingness to stay in their organisation. British Journal of Social Work. https://doi.org/10.1093/bjsw/bcz103

Ruotsalainen, S., Elovainio, M., Jantunen, S., &amp; Sinervo, T. (2023). The mediating effect of psychosocial factors in the relationship between self-organizing teams and employee wellbeing: A cross-sectional observational study. International Journal of Nursing Studies. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijnurstu.2022.104415

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#BeingAssignedToNonPreferredRoles">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a work arrangement in which an employee is repeatedly allocated to roles, tasks, or units that do not match their stated preferences.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a developer who asked for back-end work but is kept on support duty.

Synonyms: undesired role assignment; nonpreferred job assignment; unwanted work assignment; involuntary role placement; role-preference mismatch; forced reassignment.

References:

Eib, C., Bernhard-Oettel, C., Leineweber, C., &amp; Näswall, K. (2021). You can’t always get what you want: Mechanisms and consequences of intra-organizational job change among middle managers in sweden. The International Journal of Human Resource Management. https://doi.org/10.1080/09585192.2021.1886153

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#BenefitsSatisfaction">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s appraisal of the employer&apos;s indirect compensation (e.g., health plan, pension, allowances) as fair and adequate.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who values the dental plan and childcare allowance offered by the company.

Synonyms: benefit satisfaction; satisfaction with employee benefits; indirect-compensation satisfaction; fringe-benefit satisfaction; benefits contentment; Indirect Compensation Satisfaction in benefits administration; Benefits Positive Appraisal.

References:

Ho, S., Stenhouse, R., &amp; Snowden, A. (2021). ‘It was quite a shock’: A qualitative study of the impact of organisational and personal factors on newly qualified nurses&apos; experiences. Journal of Clinical Nursing. https://doi.org/10.1111/jocn.15777

Nordgren Selar, A., Gagné, M., Hellgren, J., Falkenberg, H., &amp; Sverke, M. (2023). Compensation profiles among private sector employees in Sweden: Differences in work-related and health-related outcomes. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.949711

Farooq, H., Janjua, U. I., Madni, T. M., Waheed, A., Zareei, M., &amp; Alanazi, F. (2022). Identification and Analysis of Factors Influencing Turnover Intention of Pakistan IT Professionals: An Empirical Study. IEEE Access. https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2022.3181753

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#CanceledShifts">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a scheduling arrangement in which scheduled shifts are recurrently revoked by the employer, often at short notice.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a retail worker whose weekend shifts are cancelled the day before on several occasions.

Synonyms: cancelled shifts; shift cancellation; employer-cancelled shift; revoked work shift; last-minute shift cancellation; cancelled work assignment.

References:

Abrams, L. R., Harknett, K., &amp; Schneider, D. (2022). Older workers with unpredictable schedules: Implications for well-being and job retention. The Gerontologist. https://doi.org/10.1093/geront/gnac067

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#CareerGrowth">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which an employee experiences visible advancement, skill progression, or upward mobility within the current organization.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an associate analyst promoted to senior analyst within 18 months along a structured development path.

Synonyms: career development; career advancement; career progression; professional growth; occupational development; Professional Advancement in talent development.

References:

Hu, C., Zhang, S., Chen, Y., &amp; Griggs, T. L. (2022). A meta-analytic study of subjective career plateaus. Journal of Vocational Behavior. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jvb.2021.103649

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#CareerPlateaus">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which the likelihood of further upward promotion or significant increase in responsibility within the organization is very low.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a coordinator who has held the same position for eight years with no promotion path available.

Synonyms: career plateau; career stagnation; hierarchical plateau; structural career plateau; content plateau; professional stagnation; Hierarchical Career Plateau; Career Stagnation State.

References:

Abrams, L. R., Harknett, K., &amp; Schneider, D. (2022). Older workers with unpredictable schedules: Implications for well-being and job retention. The Gerontologist. https://doi.org/10.1093/geront/gnac067

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Child">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Phase (anti-rigid sortal) of LivingPerson representing the initial stage of the human life cycle, before adolescence.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, persons in this phase cannot play the Employee role, which is restricted to Adolescent, Adult, or Elderly persons.

Examples include a person under the minimum legal working age.

Synonyms: minor; young person; juvenile; underage person; dependent child.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ClosingShifts">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a scheduling arrangement in which an employee is consistently assigned to the final business hours of an establishment.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a restaurant employee who always works until closing time.

Synonyms: closing-duty shift; late closing shift; end-of-day shift; night closing shift; close shift; late shift.

References:

Astvik, W., Welander, JO., &amp; Larsson, R. (2020). Reasons for staying: A longitudinal study of work conditions predicting social workers’ willingness to stay in their organisation. British Journal of Social Work. https://doi.org/10.1093/bjsw/bcz103

Garthe, N., &amp; Hasselhorn, H. M. (2022). Changes of profession, employer and work tasks in later working life: An empirical overview of staying and leaving. Ageing &amp; Society. https://doi.org/10.1017/S0144686X21000088

Ho, S., Stenhouse, R., &amp; Snowden, A. (2021). ‘It was quite a shock’: A qualitative study of the impact of organisational and personal factors on newly qualified nurses&apos; experiences. Journal of Clinical Nursing. https://doi.org/10.1111/jocn.15777

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#CoachingProgram">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which structured coaching initiatives focused on skill development and performance are available to employees.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a company that offers each new manager six months of executive coaching.

Synonyms: Executive Coaching Initiatives; Workplace Coaching; Coaching Initiatives; Coaching Interventions; Coaching Schemes.

References:

Farooq, H., Janjua, U. I., Madni, T. M., Waheed, A., Zareei, M., &amp; Alanazi, F. (2022). Identification and Analysis of Factors Influencing Turnover Intention of Pakistan IT Professionals: An Empirical Study. IEEE Access. https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2022.3181753

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#CollectiveSocialAgent">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Category that classifies social agents composed of multiple individuals acting collectively under a shared social identity, without the formal legal constitution of an institution.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it covers informal or semi-formal collectives that may act in employment settings (for instance, as a work group raising a collective demand), as distinct from institutional agents such as juridical persons.

Examples include a group of employees organizing a collective petition or an informal professional community.

Synonyms: collective agent; organized collective; social collective; group agent; collective actor; Group Agent Category.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ConfidentLeadersInTheirRoles">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a leadership situation in which supervisors demonstrate high role efficacy, clear decision-making, and emotional steadiness.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a team led by a manager who communicates decisions clearly and handles crises calmly.

Synonyms: leader role confidence; managerial self-confidence; leadership self-efficacy; supervisory confidence; leader role efficacy; managerial confidence.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ConsecutiveShifts">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a scheduling arrangement in which an employee works multiple shifts back-to-back without the standard rest period.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a technician scheduled for a night shift immediately followed by a day shift.

Synonyms: back-to-back shifts; successive shifts; continuous shift sequence; consecutive work periods; serial shifts; double shifts.

References:

Abrams, L. R., Harknett, K., &amp; Schneider, D. (2022). Older workers with unpredictable schedules: Implications for well-being and job retention. The Gerontologist. https://doi.org/10.1093/geront/gnac067

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ContinuousAssessmentOfPerson">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which employees receive ongoing, constructive feedback and periodic evaluations rather than a single annual appraisal.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a company that holds quarterly feedback conversations for every employee.

Synonyms: continuous performance assessment; ongoing employee evaluation; continuous performance management; ongoing appraisal; continuous feedback; frequent performance review.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#CostsOfProductsAndServicesOffered">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which the cost of producing goods or delivering services increases as a result of employee exits.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of voluntary or involuntary turnover for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerVoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext or an EmployerInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by the corresponding VoluntaryTurnover or InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a company whose unit service cost rises after losing experienced staff.

Synonyms: Cost of Goods Sold (COGS) Impact; Service Delivery Costs; Expenses of Products and Services Offered; Expenditures of Products and Services Offered; Outlays of Products and Services Offered.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#CounselingProgram">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which employees have confidential access to professional psychological counseling or an employee assistance program.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an organization that provides free, confidential sessions with a psychologist.

Synonyms: Employee Assistance Program (EAP); Workplace Psychological Support; Counseling Initiatives; Counseling Interventions; Counseling Schemes.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#CumulativeCostIncrease">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which operational and replacement costs escalate over time due to employee exits.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of voluntary or involuntary turnover for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerVoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext or an EmployerInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by the corresponding VoluntaryTurnover or InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a company whose replacement expenses grow year after year.

Synonyms: Escalating Turnover Overhead; Cumulative Expenses Increase; Cumulative Expenditures Increase; Cumulative Outlays Increase.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#DamageToEmployabilityPotential">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which a dismissed person&apos;s market value or attractiveness to future employers is diminished.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of involuntary turnover for the employee. Its instances are elements of an EmployeeInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by an InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a professional who struggles to find a new job after a dismissal.

Synonyms: Employability Degradation; Labor Market Value Loss; Harm to Employability Potential; Impairment of Employability Potential; Erosion of Employability Potential; Loss of Employability Potential.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#DamageToProfessionalSelfEsteemAndIdentity">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which a dismissed person&apos;s confidence in their skills and professional identity is harmed.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of involuntary turnover for the employee. Its instances are elements of an EmployeeInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by an InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a manager who questions their competence after being dismissed.

Synonyms: Professional Self-Worth Erosion; Occupational Identity Crisis; Harm to Professional Self Esteem and Identity; Impairment of Professional Self Esteem and Identity; Erosion of Professional Self Esteem and Identity; Loss of Professional Self Esteem and Identity.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#DeceasedPerson">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Phase (anti-rigid sortal) of Person representing a human being after biological death.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, the transition to this phase ends every role and relator in which the person participated, including Employment.

Examples include an employee whose employment relationship ends due to death.

Synonyms: dead person; decedent; late individual; nonliving person.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Decision">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode that specializes Intention and represents a commitment established through deliberation, as in the core ontology on decision making.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, a Decision is created in a DecisionMaking event and may be manifested in a DecisionResultingAction. It is the supertype of DecisionToLeave, DecisionToStay, and DecisionToTerminate.

Examples include an employee&apos;s decision to resign or an employer&apos;s decision to dismiss.

Synonyms: choice; resolution; commitment; deliberative outcome; intentional commitment; Decision commitment; Decision deliberative outcome; Decision choice; Decision intentional resolution; Decision decision state.

References:

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf

Guizzardi, R., Perini, A., &amp; Susi, A. (2018). Aligning Goal and Decision Modeling. In Information Systems in the Big Data Era (pp. 124–132). https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-92901-9_12

Guizzardi, G., Benevides, A. B., Fonseca, C. M., Porello, D., Almeida, J. P. A., &amp; Sales, T. P. (2022). UFO: Unified Foundational Ontology. Applied Ontology, 17(1), 167–210. https://doi.org/10.3233/AO-210256

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#DecisionMaking">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Event type, specialization of Action, representing a deliberative act in which an agent weighs intentions, beliefs, preferences, and contextual conditions, resulting in the creation of a Decision.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, an intention is manifested in a DecisionMaking event, and the resulting Decision was created in it. Its subtypes (Leave, Stay, and TerminationDecisionMakingEvent) are pairwise disjoint.

Examples include an employee comparing an external offer with their current job.

Synonyms: deliberation; choice process; decision process; decision formation; evaluative deliberation; Decision Making commitment; Decision Making deliberative outcome; Decision Making choice; Decision Making intentional resolution; Decision Making decision state.

References:

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. In Proceedings of the XIII Seminar on Ontology Research in Brazil and IV Doctoral and Masters Consortium on Ontologies (ONTOBRAS 2020) (CEUR Workshop Proceedings, Vol. 2728). CEUR-WS.org.

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#DecisionResultingAction">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Event type, specialization of Action, representing an action that manifests a previously established Decision.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it is the common supertype of Turnover and Retention and brings about consequence contexts (gufo:broughtAbout). It is disjoint from DecisionMaking.

Examples include an employee&apos;s resignation or a company&apos;s decision to keep an employee through a counteroffer.

Synonyms: decision-manifesting action; decision-based action; decision-enacting action; chosen action; action resulting from a decision; decision implementation; Decision Resulting Action commitment; Decision Resulting Action deliberative outcome; Decision Resulting Action choice; Decision Resulting Action intentional resolution; Decision Resulting Action decision state.

References:

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf

Guizzardi, G., Benevides, A. B., Fonseca, C. M., Porello, D., Almeida, J. P. A., &amp; Sales, T. P. (2022). UFO: Unified Foundational Ontology. Applied Ontology, 17(1), 167–210. https://doi.org/10.3233/AO-210256

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#DecisionToLeave">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode, both a Decision and an IntentionToLeave, representing an employee&apos;s deliberatively established commitment to leave the employing organization.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it is created in a LeaveDecisionMakingEvent and may be manifested in a VoluntaryTurnover. The inherence restriction uses &apos;some&apos; because gufo:inheresIn is functional, which makes &apos;min 1&apos; redundant.

Examples include an employee who, after weighing an external offer, decides to resign.

Synonyms: leaving decision; exit decision; decision to quit; decision to resign; separation decision; decision to depart; Decision To Leave commitment; Decision To Leave deliberative outcome; Decision To Leave choice; Decision To Leave intentional resolution; Decision To Leave decision state.

References:

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf

Guizzardi, R., Perini, A., &amp; Susi, A. (2018). Aligning Goal and Decision Modeling. In Information Systems in the Big Data Era (pp. 124–132). https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-92901-9_12

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Master&apos;s thesis, Lund University]. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#DecisionToStay">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode, both a Decision and an IntentionToStay, representing an employee&apos;s deliberatively established commitment to remain with the employing organization.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it is created in a StayDecisionMakingEvent and may be manifested in a Retention. The inherence restriction uses &apos;some&apos; because gufo:inheresIn is functional, which makes &apos;min 1&apos; redundant.

Examples include an employee who declines an external offer and decides to stay.

Synonyms: staying decision; retention decision; decision to remain; continuance decision; decision to continue employment; Decision To Stay commitment; Decision To Stay deliberative outcome; Decision To Stay choice; Decision To Stay intentional resolution; Decision To Stay decision state.

References:

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf

Guizzardi, R., Perini, A., &amp; Susi, A. (2018). Aligning Goal and Decision Modeling. In Information Systems in the Big Data Era (pp. 124–132). https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-92901-9_12

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Master&apos;s thesis, Lund University]. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#DecisionToTerminate">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode, both a Decision and an IntentionToTerminate, representing an employer&apos;s deliberatively established commitment to terminate an employment relationship.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it is created in a TerminationDecisionMakingEvent and may be manifested in a InvoluntaryTurnover. The inherence restriction uses &apos;some&apos; because gufo:inheresIn is functional, which makes &apos;min 1&apos; redundant.

Examples include a company that, after a performance review, decides to dismiss an employee.

Synonyms: termination decision; dismissal decision; discharge decision; decision to end employment; employer separation decision; Decision To Terminate commitment; Decision To Terminate deliberative outcome; Decision To Terminate choice; Decision To Terminate intentional resolution; Decision To Terminate decision state.

References:

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf

Guizzardi, R., Perini, A., &amp; Susi, A. (2018). Aligning Goal and Decision Modeling. In Information Systems in the Big Data Era (pp. 124–132). https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-92901-9_12

Santana, A. C., Rodrigues, C. M. de O., Farias Junior, I. H. de, &amp; Santos, W. B. (2023). OntoTurnover: A lightweight domain ontology for modeling employee turnover. In 2023 18th Iberian Conference on Information Systems and Technologies (CISTI) (pp. 1–4). IEEE. https://doi.org/10.23919/CISTI58278.2023.10211799

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#DeclineInOrganizationalPerformance">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state of diminished business efficiency, sales, or productivity following employee exits.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of voluntary or involuntary turnover for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerVoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext or an EmployerInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by the corresponding VoluntaryTurnover or InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a branch whose sales fall after several key salespeople leave.

Synonyms: Decline; Diminished Corporate Output; Performance Degradation; Decline in Workplace Performance; Decline in Institutional Performance; Decline in Company Performance; Decline in Organizational Effectiveness; Decline in Organizational Productivity; Decline in Organizational Work Performance.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#DelaysInProjectAndDeliveryDeadlines">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which projects or deliveries are completed past their deadlines due to employee exits.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of voluntary or involuntary turnover for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerVoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext or an EmployerInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by the corresponding VoluntaryTurnover or InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a software release postponed after the departure of two developers.

Synonyms: project delivery delays; schedule slippage; delivery slippage; project delays; delayed milestones; deadline overruns; Missed Milestones in project management metrics.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#DifficultRelationshipWithTheLeader">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a relational situation characterized by low trust, poor communication, or recurrent friction between an employee and their immediate leader.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include an employee whose manager frequently dismisses their input in meetings.

Synonyms: poor supervisor relationship; leader–member relationship difficulty; supervisor–subordinate friction; strained manager relationship; low-quality leader–member exchange; manager–employee conflict; Low-Quality Leadership Dyad.

References:

Di Stefano, G., Venza, G., &amp; Aiello, D. (2020). Associations of job insecurity with perceived work-related symptoms, job satisfaction, and turnover intentions: The mediating role of leader–member exchange and the moderating role of organizational support. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.01329

Farooq, H., Janjua, U. I., Madni, T. M., Waheed, A., Zareei, M., &amp; Alanazi, F. (2022). Identification and Analysis of Factors Influencing Turnover Intention of Pakistan IT Professionals: An Empirical Study. IEEE Access. https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2022.3181753

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Dismissal">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Event type representing an individual, employer-initiated termination, usually motivated by causes specific to the employee.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it is the individual form of InvoluntaryTurnover and is disjoint from Layoff.

Examples include an employee dismissed after repeated misconduct.

Synonyms: discharge; termination; firing; employer-initiated separation; involuntary separation; removal from employment.

References:

Eib, C., Bernhard-Oettel, C., Leineweber, C., &amp; Näswall, K. (2021). You can’t always get what you want: Mechanisms and consequences of intra-organizational job change among middle managers in sweden. The International Journal of Human Resource Management. https://doi.org/10.1080/09585192.2021.1886153

Farooq, H., Janjua, U. I., Madni, T. M., Waheed, A., Zareei, M., &amp; Alanazi, F. (2022). Identification and Analysis of Factors Influencing Turnover Intention of Pakistan IT Professionals: An Empirical Study. IEEE Access. https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2022.3181753

Nazir, T., Umer, M., Najam, M., Nawab, S., Maqsoom, A., Shafi, K., Munir, Y., &amp; Nawaz, I. (2022). Impact role stress on turnover intentions of Pakistan’s healthcare workers: Mediating and moderating role of organizational cynicism and self-efficacy. PLoS ONE. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0279075

Palanci, Y., Mengenci, C., Bayraktaroğlu, S., &amp; Emhan, A. (2021). Analysis of workplace health and safety, job stress, interpersonal conflict, and turnover intention: A comparative study in the health sector. Health Psychology Report. https://doi.org/10.5114/hpr.2020.99971

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#DistributedLeadership">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which decision-making authority and leadership responsibilities are shared across team members.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a product team in which different members lead different workstreams.

Synonyms: Shared Leadership; Decentralized Management; Distributed Supervision; Distributed Management; Distributed Leader Behavior.

References:

Quek, S. J., Thomson, L., Houghton, R., Bramley, L., Davis, S., &amp; Cooper, J. (2021). Distributed leadership as a predictor of employee engagement, job satisfaction and turnover intention in UK nursing staff. Journal of Nursing Management. https://doi.org/10.1111/jonm.13321

Barattucci, M., Teresi, M., Pietroni, D., Iacobucci, S., Lo Presti, A., &amp; Pagliaro, S. (2021). Ethical climate(s), distributed leadership, and work outcomes: The mediating role of organizational identification. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.564112

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#DropInTeamMorale">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in a work group of reduced motivation and emotional detachment following colleagues&apos; exits.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of voluntary or involuntary turnover for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerVoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext or an EmployerInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by the corresponding VoluntaryTurnover or InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a team demotivated after several colleagues are dismissed.

Synonyms: decline in team morale; reduced group morale; deterioration of team spirit; lower workforce morale; team demoralization; erosion of team morale.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#DropInTeamProductivity">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which a team&apos;s output and delivery speed decline due to staffing instability.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of voluntary or involuntary turnover for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerVoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext or an EmployerInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by the corresponding VoluntaryTurnover or InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a team whose weekly output falls after losing two members.

Synonyms: decline in team productivity; reduced team output; lower group performance; diminished team efficiency; team performance loss; productivity deterioration.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#DropInTheQualityOfCustomerService">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which customer support, responsiveness, or satisfaction decline due to employee exits.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of voluntary or involuntary turnover for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerVoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext or an EmployerInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by the corresponding VoluntaryTurnover or InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a call center whose satisfaction scores drop after high attrition.

Synonyms: decline in customer-service quality; customer-service deterioration; reduced service quality; customer support degradation; lower customer-care quality; service-performance decline.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#EconomicInsecurity">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing an economic situation of an employee characterized by unpredictable income and lack of financial stability.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a commission-based salesperson whose monthly income varies widely.

Synonyms: Financial Precarity; Economic Anxiety State; Economic Uncertainty; Economic Lack of Security.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#EconomicSafety">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing an economic situation of an employee characterized by financial predictability, competitive fixed pay, and low risk of sudden income loss.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee with a stable salary above the market median and a permanent contract.

Synonyms: Financial Security; Economic Stability in labor market studies; Economic Security; Economic Safety Climate.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Elderly">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Phase (anti-rigid sortal) of LivingPerson representing the late stage of the human life cycle.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, elderly persons may still play the Employee role or the RetiredPerson role; age phases are independent of these roles.

Examples include a 68-year-old consultant still employed, or a retired former employee.

Synonyms: older adult; older person; senior adult; aged person; later-life adult.

References:

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Fonseca, C. M. (2026). gUFO: A gentle foundational ontology for Semantic Web knowledge graphs. Applied Ontology.

Garthe, N., &amp; Hasselhorn, H. M. (2022). Changes of profession, employer and work tasks in later working life: An empirical overview of staying and leaving. Ageing &amp; Society.

Santana, A. C. (2024). OntoTurnover: Uma Abordagem Ontológica para Compreender a Rotatividade de Profissionais (Master&apos;s dissertation). Universidade de Pernambuco.

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#EmotionalIntelligence">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s capability to recognize, understand, and manage emotions in oneself and in others.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a team member who de-escalates conflicts by acknowledging colleagues&apos; frustrations.

Synonyms: emotional quotient; EQ; emotional competence; emotional ability; emotion-related intelligence; ability emotional intelligence; Emotional Competency.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Employee">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Role (anti-rigid, relationally dependent sortal) played by a living person when performing work under an employment relationship.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, the role is founded on the Employment relator (inverse of gufo:mediates). Employee-side intentions (IntentionToLeave, IntentionToStay), decisions, and psychological factors of turnover and retention inhere in persons playing this role.

Examples include a software engineer hired by a technology company or a nurse employed by a hospital.

Synonyms: worker; staff member; member of staff; wage earner; employed person; jobholder; incumbent; Staff; Workforce.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#EmployeeCommitment">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s psychological attachment and loyalty to the organization, in its affective, normative, or continuance forms.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who stays because they feel emotionally attached to the company.

Synonyms: organizational commitment; employee loyalty; organizational attachment; work commitment; commitment to the organization; Worker Commitment; Staff Commitment; Workforce Commitment; Employee Attachment; Employee Dedication.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#EmployeeWellBeing">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s overall state of physical, psychological, and social health.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who reports good sleep, low stress, and positive relationships at work.

Synonyms: employee wellbeing; worker well-being; occupational well-being; workplace well-being; employee wellness; work-related well-being; Employee Health State; Staff Well Being; Workforce Well Being; Employee Welfare.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Employer">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:RoleMixin (anti-rigid, relationally dependent non-sortal) played by an agent that contracts and uses labor under an employment relationship.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it is a mixin because it can be played by a JuridicalPerson (a company) or by a Person (an individual employer). The role is founded on the Employment relator, and the IntentionToTerminate inheres in the agent playing it.

Examples include a technology corporation hiring engineers, or a homeowner employing a domestic worker.

Synonyms: Also termed &quot;Employing Entity&quot;, &quot;Company&quot; or &quot;Contracting Party&quot; in employment law.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Employment">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Relator (a relator kind) representing the legal, social, and economic bond that mediates an employee and an employer.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, Employment is the foundation of the Employee and Employer roles. Turnover events terminate it (gufo:wasTerminatedIn), while retention corresponds to its continuation.

Examples include an indefinite-term employment contract between an analyst and a bank.

Synonyms: employment relationship; work relationship; employment engagement; jobholding; paid work; employer-employee relationship.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#EthicalClimateOfCollaboration">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing an organizational climate characterized by mutual trust, transparent communication, and cooperative norms.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a department in which information is shared openly and credit is distributed fairly.

Synonyms: Collaborative Ethical Culture; Trust-Based Work Climate in business ethics; Ethical Environment of Collaboration; Ethical Atmosphere of Collaboration; Ethical Work Climate of Collaboration.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#EthicalLeadership">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a leadership situation in which leaders demonstrate high moral standards, fairness, and transparency in decision-making.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a director who explains the criteria behind every promotion decision.

Synonyms: Moral Leadership; Fair Supervision in leadership ethics; Ethical Supervision; Ethical Management; Ethical Leader Behavior.

References:

Niinihuhta, M., &amp; Häggman-Laitila, A. (2022). A systematic review of the relationships between nurse leaders&apos; leadership styles and nurses&apos; work-related well-being. International Journal of Nursing Practice. https://doi.org/10.1111/ijn.13040

Barattucci, M., Teresi, M., Pietroni, D., Iacobucci, S., Lo Presti, A., &amp; Pagliaro, S. (2021). Ethical climate(s), distributed leadership, and work outcomes: The mediating role of organizational identification. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.564112

Mostafa, A. M. S., Farley, S., &amp; Zaharie, M. (2021). Examining the boundaries of ethical leadership: The harmful effect of co-worker social undermining on disengagement and employee attitudes. Journal of Business Ethics. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10551-020-04586-2

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ExcessOfExtraHours">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which an employee is consistently required or pressured to work overtime well beyond contracted hours.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include an analyst who works an average of 15 extra hours per week during the whole year.

Synonyms: excessive overtime; long overtime hours; extended working hours; overwork; excess work hours; overtime overload; Overwork Hours State.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ExposureToBullying">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a social situation in which an employee is repeatedly subjected to hostile, humiliating, or exclusionary behavior by colleagues or supervisors.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who is repeatedly mocked in front of the team by a senior colleague.

Synonyms: workplace bullying exposure; exposure to workplace harassment; workplace victimization; repeated mistreatment at work; occupational bullying; psychological harassment exposure; Psychological Harassment State.

References:

Glambek, M., Einarsen, S. V., Gjerstad, J., &amp; Nielsen, M. B. (2023). Last in, first out? Length of service as a moderator of the relationship between exposure to bullying behaviors and work-related outcomes. Current Psychology: A Journal for Diverse Perspectives on Diverse Psychological Issues. https://doi.org/10.1007/s12144-022-04202-8

Sischka, P. E., Melzer, A., Schmidt, A. F., &amp; Steffgen, G. (2021). Psychological contract violation or basic need frustration? Psychological mechanisms behind the effects of workplace bullying. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.627968

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ExtinctOrganization">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Phase (anti-rigid sortal) of JuridicalPerson representing the dissolved or legally terminated status of an organization.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, the transition to this phase terminates the organization&apos;s capacity to maintain employment relationships; the phase is disjoint from ActiveOrganization.

Examples include a company closed after bankruptcy or merger into another entity.

Synonyms: Also termed &quot;Dissolved Company&quot; or &quot;Defunct Entity&quot; in corporate registries.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ExtraWorkload">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which an employee is assigned a volume of tasks or responsibilities that exceeds the standard for the role.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who absorbs the tasks of a colleague who left and was not replaced.

Synonyms: Additional Work Burden; Excessive Task Allocation; Extra Work Demands; Extra Job Demands; Extra Task Load.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#FinancialInstabilityAndInsecurity">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which a dismissed person lacks financial predictability or steady income.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of involuntary turnover for the employee. Its instances are elements of an EmployeeInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by an InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a dismissed worker who depends on severance and savings.

Synonyms: Economic Precarity; Income Volatility; Financial Instability and Uncertainty; Financial Instability and Lack of Security.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Flexibility">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a work arrangement in which employees have control over their working hours, schedule adjustments, or place of work.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who can start work between 7 and 10 a.m. and work from home twice a week.

Synonyms: Schedule Flexibility; Workplace Adaptability in job characteristics research; Work Flexibility; Adaptability; Discretion.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#GiveImportanceToTheEmployeeComplaintsSystems">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which employee grievances and formal complaints are handled seriously, confidentially, and with follow-up.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a company whose ombudsperson channel responds to every complaint within ten days.

Synonyms: Grievance System Responsiveness; Employee Voice Protection in industrial relations; Give Importance to the Worker Complaints Systems; Give Importance to the Staff Complaints Systems; Give Importance to the Workforce Complaints Systems.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#GoodRecruitmentProcess">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which recruitment is transparent and realistic, aligning candidates&apos; expectations with the actual role.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a hiring process that includes a realistic job preview and meetings with future peers.

Synonyms: effective recruitment process; high-quality hiring process; sound recruitment practice; fair selection process; realistic recruitment; effective talent acquisition.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#GoodRelationshipWithLeadership">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a relational situation characterized by mutual trust, high-quality exchange, and open communication between an employee and their leader.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who has regular, candid conversations with a supportive manager.

Synonyms: High Leader-Member Exchange (LMX); Positive Supervisor Relations; Good Relationship with Supervision; Good Relationship with Management; Good Relationship with Leader Behavior.

References:

Di Stefano, G., Venza, G., &amp; Aiello, D. (2020). Associations of job insecurity with perceived work-related symptoms, job satisfaction, and turnover intentions: The mediating role of leader–member exchange and the moderating role of organizational support. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.01329

Farooq, H., Janjua, U. I., Madni, T. M., Waheed, A., Zareei, M., &amp; Alanazi, F. (2022). Identification and Analysis of Factors Influencing Turnover Intention of Pakistan IT Professionals: An Empirical Study. IEEE Access. https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2022.3181753

Ho, S., Stenhouse, R., &amp; Snowden, A. (2021). ‘It was quite a shock’: A qualitative study of the impact of organisational and personal factors on newly qualified nurses&apos; experiences. Journal of Clinical Nursing. https://doi.org/10.1111/jocn.15777

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Gossip">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a social situation in the workplace characterized by the spreading of personal, unverified, or derogatory rumors about employees.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a team in which rumors about colleagues&apos; private lives circulate frequently.

Synonyms: workplace gossip; organizational gossip; rumor spreading; informal workplace rumors; employee gossip; grapevine talk; Spreading of Rumors; Toxic Workplace Chat.

References:

Georganta, K., &amp; Montgomery, A. (2022). Workplace fun is not enough: The role of work engagement and trust. Cogent Psychology. https://doi.org/10.1080/23311908.2022.2060603

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#HealthyProfessionalEnvironment">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a workplace situation characterized by physical safety, ergonomic design, and a positive psychosocial environment.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an office with ergonomic workstations and an active occupational health program.

Synonyms: healthy work environment; healthy workplace; supportive work environment; positive workplace climate; health-promoting workplace; safe professional environment.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#HighLevelsOfOrganizationalTurnover">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing an organizational situation characterized by a high frequency of employee exits and replacements.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a unit that replaces a third of its staff every year.

Synonyms: High Workforce Attrition; Systemic Turnover Environment; Elevated Levels of Organizational Turnover; Strong Levels of Organizational Turnover; High Level of Levels of Organizational Turnover; Increased Levels of Organizational Turnover; High Levels of Workplace Turnover; High Levels of Institutional Turnover; High Levels of Company Turnover.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#HighPerceivedInternalEmployability">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s perception of having good opportunities for promotion, rotation, and development within the current organization.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who sees several internal openings compatible with their profile.

Synonyms: Internal Career Efficacy; Perceived Internal Job Mobility; Elevated Perceived Internal Employability; Strong Perceived Internal Employability; High Level of Perceived Internal Employability; Increased Perceived Internal Employability; High Subjective Internal Employability; High Experienced Internal Employability; High Perceived Level of Internal Employability.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#HighPerceivedQualityOfTheServiceDelivered">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s perception that the products or services delivered by their team or organization are of high quality.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a nurse who is proud of the quality of care provided by the ward.

Synonyms: Internal Career Efficacy; Perceived Internal Job Mobility; Elevated Perceived Quality of the Service Delivered; Strong Perceived Quality of the Service Delivered; High Level of Perceived Quality of the Service Delivered; Increased Perceived Quality of the Service Delivered; High Subjective Quality of the Service Delivered; High Experienced Quality of the Service Delivered; High Perceived Level of Quality of the Service Delivered.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#HindranceStressors">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a work situation characterized by demands or obstacles perceived as unnecessarily hindering goal achievement, such as red tape or role ambiguity.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include an employee whose projects are repeatedly stalled by unclear approval chains.

Synonyms: hindrance demands; obstructive job stressors; work barriers; dysfunctional stressors; constraint stressors; job hindrances; Work Obstacles.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#HiringCosts">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state of expenses associated with contracting and administratively processing new employees to replace those who left.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of voluntary or involuntary turnover for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerVoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext or an EmployerInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by the corresponding VoluntaryTurnover or InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include the administrative cost of hiring replacements after a wave of exits.

Synonyms: Onboarding Expenses; New Hire Administrative Costs; Hiring Expenses; Hiring Expenditures; Hiring Outlays.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#HumanResourceOrientation">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing an organizational situation characterized by people-centered and supportive HR policies and strategies.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a company that consults employees before changing benefits.

Synonyms: employee-oriented HR philosophy; people-oriented management; human-centered HR orientation; employee-centered HRM; people-first orientation; supportive HR orientation.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#IdiosyncraticDeals">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a work arrangement negotiated individually between an employee and the employer to fit the employee&apos;s specific needs.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who negotiated a four-day week to pursue a master&apos;s degree.

Synonyms: i-deals; idiosyncratic work arrangements; personalized work arrangements; individually negotiated employment terms; customized employment deals; personalized employment conditions.

References:

Zhang, X., Deng, H., Xia, Y., &amp; Lan, Y. (2021). Employability paradox: The effect of development idiosyncratic deals on recipient employees’ turnover intention. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.696309

Chênevert, D., Hill, K., &amp; Kilroy, S. (2022). Employees perceptions of non‐monetary recognition practice and turnover: Does recognition source alignment and contrast matter? Human Resource Management Journal. https://doi.org/10.1111/1748-8583.12354

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#IncreaseInOrganizationalPerformance">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state of enhanced productivity and achievement of business goals associated with employee retention.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of retention for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerRetentionConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by a Retention. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a unit whose productivity rises after two years of low attrition.

Synonyms: Increase; Enhanced Corporate Performance; Productivity Gains; Increased Organizational Performance; Growth in Organizational Performance; Rise in Organizational Performance; Enhancement of Organizational Performance; Increase in Workplace Performance; Increase in Institutional Performance; Increase in Company Performance; Increase in Organizational Effectiveness; Increase in Organizational Productivity.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#IncreaseOfIntentionToChangeIndustry">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which a dismissed person shows an increased intention to leave their industry.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of involuntary turnover for the employee. Its instances are elements of an EmployeeInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by an InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a dismissed banker who considers moving to another sector.

Synonyms: Escalated Industry Attrition Risk; Increased Intention to Change Industry; Growth in Intention to Change Industry; Rise in Intention to Change Industry; Enhancement of Intention to Change Industry.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#IncreasedWorkloadOfRemainingEmployees">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which the tasks of departed employees are redistributed to the remaining staff.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of voluntary or involuntary turnover for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerVoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext or an EmployerInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by the corresponding VoluntaryTurnover or InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include remaining team members who absorb the tasks of dismissed colleagues.

Synonyms: Workload Inflation for Surviving Staff; Increased Work Demands of Remaining Employees; Increased Job Demands of Remaining Employees; Increased Task Load of Remaining Employees.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#IndividualisticEthicalClimate">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing an organizational climate characterized by self-interest, intense internal competition, and lack of mutual support.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a sales unit in which colleagues withhold leads from each other.

Synonyms: Egoistic Ethical Climate; Hyper-Competitive Work Culture; Individualistic Ethical Environment; Individualistic Ethical Atmosphere; Individualistic Ethical Work Climate.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#InstitutionalAgent">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Category (rigid non-sortal) that classifies formally structured organizations and institutional bodies capable of acting as social agents.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it is the supertype of JuridicalPerson and the institutional side of employment and retirement: organizations that employ people and institutions that grant retirement benefits are institutional agents.

Examples include companies, public agencies, and social security institutions.

Synonyms: Also termed &quot;Institutional Entity&quot; or &quot;Formal Organization Category&quot;.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#IntegrationCosts">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state of resources and time required for replacement employees to reach full productivity.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of voluntary or involuntary turnover for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerVoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext or an EmployerInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by the corresponding VoluntaryTurnover or InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include productivity lost while new hires learn internal systems.

Synonyms: Assimilation Costs; Productivity Ramp-Up Costs; Integration Expenses; Integration Expenditures; Integration Outlays.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Intention">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode (a mode kind) representing an intentional mental commitment of an agent directed toward a goal, outcome, or course of action.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, Intention is the common supertype of the turnover and retention intentions and of Decision. Intentions are not triggered by contextual factors; they are created in an IntentionFormation event (gufo:wasCreatedIn), which is triggered by a TurnoverContext.

Examples include an employee&apos;s intention to resign or an employer&apos;s intention to dismiss.

Synonyms: Behavioral Intent, Intentional Moment, Mental Mode, Psychological Driver.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Santana, A. C., Rodrigues, C. M. de O., Farias Junior, I. H. de, &amp; Santos, W. B. (2023). OntoTurnover: A lightweight domain ontology for modeling employee turnover. In 2023 18th Iberian Conference on Information Systems and Technologies (CISTI) (pp. 1–4). IEEE. https://doi.org/10.23919/CISTI58278.2023.10211799

Santana, A. C., Farias Junior, I. H. de, Santos, W. B., &amp; Rodrigues, C. M. de O. (2023). OntoTurnover: Uma ontologia de domínio sobre a rotatividade de profissionais. In Anais Estendidos do XXII Simpósio Brasileiro de Qualidade de Software (pp. 13–18). Sociedade Brasileira de Computação. https://doi.org/10.5753/sbqsestendido.2023.235887</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#IntentionFormation">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:EventType that classifies the mental events in which an intention comes into existence, prior to and independent of any deliberation.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, IntentionFormation connects contextual factors to intentions: a TurnoverContext contributes to triggering it (gufo:contributedToTrigger) and the intention was created in it (gufo:wasCreatedIn). The property chain :contributedToFormationOf composes these links, so the reasoner infers which factors contributed to the formation of which intention, whatever the nature of the factor.

Examples include the moment in which an employee, after a sequence of negative experiences, first forms the intention to leave.

Synonyms: intention onset; intention emergence; formation of withdrawal cognitions.

References:

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf

Mobley, W. H. (1977). Intermediate linkages in the relationship between job satisfaction and employee turnover. Journal of Applied Psychology, 62(2), 237–240.</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#IntentionToChangeCareer">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode inhering in an Employee that represents the employee&apos;s commitment to abandon their current occupation.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it specializes IntentionToLeave and inherits its creation, inherence, and manifestation constraints. The four subtypes of IntentionToLeave are pairwise disjoint.

Examples include a lawyer who plans to become a teacher.

Synonyms: Also termed &quot;Occupational Turnover Intention&quot; or &quot;Career Change Intention&quot; in career counseling.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Santana, A. C., Rodrigues, C. M. de O., Farias Junior, I. H. de, &amp; Santos, W. B. (2023). OntoTurnover: A lightweight domain ontology for modeling employee turnover. In 2023 18th Iberian Conference on Information Systems and Technologies (CISTI) (pp. 1–4). IEEE. https://doi.org/10.23919/CISTI58278.2023.10211799

Santana, A. C., Farias Junior, I. H. de, Santos, W. B., &amp; Rodrigues, C. M. de O. (2023). OntoTurnover: Uma ontologia de domínio sobre a rotatividade de profissionais. In Anais Estendidos do XXII Simpósio Brasileiro de Qualidade de Software (pp. 13–18). Sociedade Brasileira de Computação. https://doi.org/10.5753/sbqsestendido.2023.235887</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#IntentionToChangeIndustry">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode inhering in an Employee that represents the employee&apos;s commitment to leave not only the current employer but the entire industry sector.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it specializes IntentionToLeave and inherits its creation, inherence, and manifestation constraints. The four subtypes of IntentionToLeave are pairwise disjoint.

Examples include a banker who plans to move to the technology sector.

Synonyms: Also termed &quot;Industry Turnover Intention&quot; or &quot;Sector Career Pivot Intention&quot; in vocational psychology.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Santana, A. C., Rodrigues, C. M. de O., Farias Junior, I. H. de, &amp; Santos, W. B. (2023). OntoTurnover: A lightweight domain ontology for modeling employee turnover. In 2023 18th Iberian Conference on Information Systems and Technologies (CISTI) (pp. 1–4). IEEE. https://doi.org/10.23919/CISTI58278.2023.10211799

Santana, A. C., Farias Junior, I. H. de, Santos, W. B., &amp; Rodrigues, C. M. de O. (2023). OntoTurnover: Uma ontologia de domínio sobre a rotatividade de profissionais. In Anais Estendidos do XXII Simpósio Brasileiro de Qualidade de Software (pp. 13–18). Sociedade Brasileira de Computação. https://doi.org/10.5753/sbqsestendido.2023.235887</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#IntentionToLeave">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode inhering in an Employee that represents the employee&apos;s commitment toward leaving the employing organization.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it is created in a LeaveIntentionFormation event triggered by a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext, and it may be manifested in a LeaveDecisionMakingEvent. When deliberation creates a DecisionToLeave (a subtype), the decision may be manifested in a VoluntaryTurnover. The union in gufo:manifestedIn keeps both manifestations valid for the subtype.

Examples include an analyst who has started thinking seriously about resigning after months of overload.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. In Proceedings of the XIII Seminar on Ontology Research in Brazil and IV Doctoral and Masters Consortium on Ontologies (ONTOBRAS 2020) (CEUR Workshop Proceedings, Vol. 2728). CEUR-WS.org.

Santana, A. C., Rodrigues, C. M. de O., Farias Junior, I. H. de, &amp; Santos, W. B. (2023). OntoTurnover: A lightweight domain ontology for modeling employee turnover. In 2023 18th Iberian Conference on Information Systems and Technologies (CISTI) (pp. 1–4). IEEE. https://doi.org/10.23919/CISTI58278.2023.10211799

Santana, A. C., Farias Junior, I. H. de, Santos, W. B., &amp; Rodrigues, C. M. de O. (2023). OntoTurnover: Uma ontologia de domínio sobre a rotatividade de profissionais. In Anais Estendidos do XXII Simpósio Brasileiro de Qualidade de Software (pp. 13–18). Sociedade Brasileira de Computação. https://doi.org/10.5753/sbqsestendido.2023.235887</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#IntentionToLeaveTheEmployer">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode inhering in an Employee that represents the employee&apos;s commitment to resign from the current employer while possibly remaining in the same industry and profession.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it specializes IntentionToLeave and inherits its creation, inherence, and manifestation constraints. The four subtypes of IntentionToLeave are pairwise disjoint.

Examples include a nurse who plans to move to another hospital.

Synonyms: Also termed &quot;Company Turnover Intention&quot; or &quot;Intent to Resign&quot; in personnel research.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Santana, A. C., Rodrigues, C. M. de O., Farias Junior, I. H. de, &amp; Santos, W. B. (2023). OntoTurnover: A lightweight domain ontology for modeling employee turnover. In 2023 18th Iberian Conference on Information Systems and Technologies (CISTI) (pp. 1–4). IEEE. https://doi.org/10.23919/CISTI58278.2023.10211799

Santana, A. C., Farias Junior, I. H. de, Santos, W. B., &amp; Rodrigues, C. M. de O. (2023). OntoTurnover: Uma ontologia de domínio sobre a rotatividade de profissionais. In Anais Estendidos do XXII Simpósio Brasileiro de Qualidade de Software (pp. 13–18). Sociedade Brasileira de Computação. https://doi.org/10.5753/sbqsestendido.2023.235887</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#IntentionToRetire">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode inhering in an Employee that represents the employee&apos;s commitment to withdraw voluntarily from the workforce through retirement.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it specializes IntentionToLeave and inherits its creation, inherence, and manifestation constraints. The four subtypes of IntentionToLeave are pairwise disjoint.

Examples include a 62-year-old engineer planning to retire next year.

Synonyms: Retirement Intention, Intent to Retire, Behavioral Intent to Retire.

References:

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Fonseca, C. M. (2026). gUFO: A gentle foundational ontology for Semantic Web knowledge graphs. Applied Ontology.

Garthe, N., &amp; Hasselhorn, H. M. (2022). Changes of profession, employer and work tasks in later working life: An empirical overview of staying and leaving. Ageing &amp; Society.</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#IntentionToStay">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode inhering in an Employee that represents the employee&apos;s commitment toward remaining with the current employer.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it is created in a StayIntentionFormation event triggered by a RetentionMotivatingContext, and it may be manifested in a StayDecisionMakingEvent. The subtype DecisionToStay may be manifested in a Retention.

Examples include an employee who, after a promotion, commits to staying with the company for the next years.

Synonyms: Also termed &quot;Intent to Remain&quot; or &quot;Retention Commitment&quot; in human resource management.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#IntentionToTerminate">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing the intention of an employer to unilaterally end an employment relationship.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this mode inheres in the agent playing the Employer role (gufo:RoleMixin): a JuridicalPerson, in the case of organizations, or a Person, in the case of individual employers. When the Employer is a social agent, the intention is ascribed to the organization itself, while managers or other authorized representatives act on its behalf to form and carry it out. It is manifested in a TerminationDecisionMakingEvent, which may create a DecisionToTerminate that is in turn manifested in an InvoluntaryTurnover event.

Examples include a company&apos;s intention to dismiss an underperforming worker, formed and executed by the responsible manager, or an organization&apos;s intention to carry out a department layoff, established by a board directive.

Synonyms: Also termed &quot;Intent to Fire&quot; or &quot;Dismissal Intention&quot; in employment relations.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Guizzardi, G., Falbo, R. A., &amp; Guizzardi, R. S. S. (2008). Grounding software domain ontologies in the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO): The case of the ODE software process ontology. In Proceedings of the XI Iberoamerican Workshop on Requirements Engineering and Software Environments (IDEAS 2008).

Santana, A. C., Rodrigues, C. M. de O., Farias Junior, I. H. de, &amp; Santos, W. B. (2023). OntoTurnover: A lightweight domain ontology for modeling employee turnover. In 2023 18th Iberian Conference on Information Systems and Technologies (CISTI) (pp. 1–4). IEEE. https://doi.org/10.23919/CISTI58278.2023.10211799

Santana, A. C., Farias Junior, I. H. de, Santos, W. B., &amp; Rodrigues, C. M. de O. (2023). OntoTurnover: Uma ontologia de domínio sobre a rotatividade de profissionais. In Anais Estendidos do XXII Simpósio Brasileiro de Qualidade de Software (pp. 13–18). Sociedade Brasileira de Computação. https://doi.org/10.5753/sbqsestendido.2023.235887</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#InterpersonalConflict">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a social situation characterized by ongoing disputes or hostility between employees or work groups.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include two team members whose recurrent disputes disrupt meetings.

Synonyms: Workplace Friction; Interpersonal Disputes; Interpersonal Incompatibility; Interpersonal Tension; Interpersonal Discord.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#InterpersonalJustice">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which employees are treated with dignity, respect, and politeness by managers and authorities.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a manager who communicates negative decisions respectfully and privately.

Synonyms: interactional justice; interpersonal fairness; respectful treatment; dignity and respect; relational justice; fair interpersonal treatment.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#InvoluntaryTurnover">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Event type representing turnover in which the employment relationship is unilaterally ended by the employer.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it manifests a DecisionToTerminate and brings about one employee-side and one employer-side consequence context. Its subtypes Dismissal and Layoff are disjoint.

Examples include an individual dismissal for low performance or a collective layoff.

Synonyms: involuntary employee turnover; employer-initiated turnover; involuntary separation; employer-initiated separation; forced exit; discharge-related turnover.

References:

Eib, C., Bernhard-Oettel, C., Leineweber, C., &amp; Näswall, K. (2021). You can’t always get what you want: Mechanisms and consequences of intra-organizational job change among middle managers in sweden. The International Journal of Human Resource Management. https://doi.org/10.1080/09585192.2021.1886153

Farooq, H., Janjua, U. I., Madni, T. M., Waheed, A., Zareei, M., &amp; Alanazi, F. (2022). Identification and Analysis of Factors Influencing Turnover Intention of Pakistan IT Professionals: An Empirical Study. IEEE Access. https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2022.3181753

Nazir, T., Umer, M., Najam, M., Nawab, S., Maqsoom, A., Shafi, K., Munir, Y., &amp; Nawaz, I. (2022). Impact role stress on turnover intentions of Pakistan’s healthcare workers: Mediating and moderating role of organizational cynicism and self-efficacy. PLoS ONE. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0279075

Palanci, Y., Mengenci, C., Bayraktaroğlu, S., &amp; Emhan, A. (2021). Analysis of workplace health and safety, job stress, interpersonal conflict, and turnover intention: A comparative study in the health sector. Health Psychology Report. https://doi.org/10.5114/hpr.2020.99971

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#JobCrafting">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which an employee proactively adjusts the task, cognitive, or relational boundaries of their job.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an accountant who takes on training new hires because they enjoy teaching.

Synonyms: proactive job redesign; self-initiated job redesign; individual job redesign; job reshaping; work crafting; task crafting; Self-Initiated Task Alignment; Role Crafting; Position Crafting.

References:

Garthe, N., &amp; Hasselhorn, H. M. (2022). Changes of profession, employer and work tasks in later working life: An empirical overview of staying and leaving. Ageing &amp; Society. https://doi.org/10.1017/S0144686X21000088

Xin, X., Cai, W., Gao, X., &amp; Liu, T. (2021). Will job crafters stay or leave? The roles of organizational instrumentality and inclusive leadership. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.743828

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#JobFlourishing">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing a psychological state inhering in an employee of thriving, positive self-regard, and optimal functioning at work.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who feels energized, competent, and growing in their current role.

Synonyms: Thriving at Work; Professional Flourishing; Work Flourishing; Role Flourishing; Position Flourishing.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#JobSatisfaction">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s positive cognitive and emotional appraisal of their work experiences and job characteristics.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who enjoys their tasks and is content with pay and colleagues.

Synonyms: work satisfaction; employee job satisfaction; occupational satisfaction; satisfaction with work; job contentment; Professional Satisfaction; Role Satisfaction; Position Satisfaction; Job Positive Appraisal.

References:

Di Stefano, G., Venza, G., &amp; Aiello, D. (2020). Associations of job insecurity with perceived work-related symptoms, job satisfaction, and turnover intentions: The mediating role of leader–member exchange and the moderating role of organizational support. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.01329

Eib, C., Bernhard-Oettel, C., Leineweber, C., &amp; Näswall, K. (2021). You can’t always get what you want: Mechanisms and consequences of intra-organizational job change among middle managers in sweden. The International Journal of Human Resource Management. https://doi.org/10.1080/09585192.2021.1886153

Farooq, H., Janjua, U. I., Madni, T. M., Waheed, A., Zareei, M., &amp; Alanazi, F. (2022). Identification and Analysis of Factors Influencing Turnover Intention of Pakistan IT Professionals: An Empirical Study. IEEE Access. https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2022.3181753

Kopperud, K. H., Nerstad, C. G. L., &amp; Dysvik, A. (2020). Should I stay or should I go? The role of motivational climate and work–home spillover for turnover intentions. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.01107

Johannsen, R., &amp; Zak, P. J. (2021). The neuroscience of organizational trust and business performance: Findings from United States working adults and an intervention at an online retailer. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.579459

Pelly, D. (2023). Worker well-being and quit intentions: Is measuring job satisfaction enough? Social Indicators Research. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11205-023-03166-x

Quek, S. J., Thomson, L., Houghton, R., Bramley, L., Davis, S., &amp; Cooper, J. (2021). Distributed leadership as a predictor of employee engagement, job satisfaction and turnover intention in UK nursing staff. Journal of Nursing Management. https://doi.org/10.1111/jonm.13321

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#JobStability">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing an employment situation in which the continuity of the employee&apos;s contract is secure in the long term.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a civil servant with a tenured position.

Synonyms: Job Security; Employment Security in labor market relations; Work Stability; Role Stability; Position Stability; Job Continuity; Job Steadiness.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#JobStrain">
        <rdfs:comment>A gufo:Situation representing a work situation combining high psychological demands with low control over how work is organized and executed.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a call center agent with strict call targets and no say over breaks.

Synonyms: work strain; occupational strain; job-related strain; work stress; psychosocial job strain; High-Demand Low-Control Stress; Role Strain; Position Strain.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#JuridicalPerson">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Kind that provides the identity principle for legally recognized corporate entities.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, JuridicalPerson is the rigid base for organizations. Organizations pass through the phases ActiveOrganization and ExtinctOrganization and may play the Employer role (gufo:RoleMixin).

Examples include a limited company registered with a tax identification number, a foundation, or a public agency.

Synonyms: Also termed &quot;Legal Entity&quot;, &quot;Corporate Person&quot;, or &quot;Juridical Entity&quot; in corporate law and ontologies.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LackOfAutonomy">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a work arrangement characterized by little freedom or decision-making power for employees over how they perform their work.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include an analyst required to follow rigid scripts with no room for judgment.

Synonyms: Low Job Autonomy; Micromanaged Work Environment; Absence of Autonomy; Insufficient Autonomy; Autonomy Deficiency; Autonomy Deficit; Low Autonomy; Lack of Job Control; Lack of Self-Determination; Lack of Decision Latitude.

References:

Asakura, K., Asakura, T., Satoh, M., Watanabe, I., &amp; Hara, Y. (2020). Health indicators as moderators of occupational commitment and nurses&apos; intention to leave. Japan Journal of Nursing Science. https://doi.org/10.1111/jjns.12277

Ruotsalainen, S., Elovainio, M., Jantunen, S., &amp; Sinervo, T. (2023). The mediating effect of psychosocial factors in the relationship between self-organizing teams and employee wellbeing: A cross-sectional observational study. International Journal of Nursing Studies. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijnurstu.2022.104415

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LackOfCareerGrowth">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation characterized by the absence of clear promotion tracks, development plans, or advancement opportunities.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a company with no career plan or internal promotion policy.

Synonyms: Absence of Career Advancement; Career Plateaus State; Absence of Career Growth; Insufficient Career Growth; Career Growth Deficiency; Career Growth Deficit; Low Career Growth; Lack of Career Development; Lack of Career Advancement; Lack of Career Progression.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LackOfInterpersonalJustice">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which employees are treated without respect, politeness, or dignity by managers or the organization.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a supervisor who publicly reprimands employees.

Synonyms: Disrespectful Managerial Treatment; Low Interpersonal Fairness; Absence of Interpersonal Justice; Insufficient Interpersonal Justice; Interpersonal Justice Deficiency; Interpersonal Justice Deficit; Low Interpersonal Justice.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LackOfLeaderExpressedHumility">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a leadership situation in which leaders fail to acknowledge mistakes or value followers&apos; contributions.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a manager who never admits errors and takes credit for the team&apos;s ideas.

Synonyms: Arrogant Supervision; Low Managerial Humility; Absence of Leader Expressed Humility; Insufficient Leader Expressed Humility; Leader Expressed Humility Deficiency; Leader Expressed Humility Deficit; Low Leader Expressed Humility.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LackOfLearningOpportunities">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which employees have limited or no access to training, development, or stimulating work.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a role with no training budget and highly repetitive tasks.

Synonyms: Skill Stagnation environment; Lack of Skill Development; Absence of Learning Opportunities; Insufficient Learning Opportunities; Learning Opportunities Deficiency; Learning Opportunities Deficit; Low Learning Opportunities; Lack of Learning Prospects; Lack of Learning Possibilities; Lack of Learning Chances.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LackOfManagementSupport">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which managers do not provide the resources, guidance, or backing employees need to handle job demands.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a team left without guidance during a major system migration.

Synonyms: Unsupportive Management; Low Supervisor Support; Absence of Management Support; Insufficient Management Support; Management Support Deficiency; Management Support Deficit; Low Management Support; Lack of Management Assistance; Lack of Management Backing; Lack of Management Help.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LackOfNonMonetaryRecognitionFromTheSupervisor">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which the immediate supervisor fails to provide praise, appreciation, or positive feedback for good work.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include an employee whose extra effort on a critical delivery goes unacknowledged.

Synonyms: Low Supervisor Recognition; Absence of Appreciation; Absence of Non Monetary Recognition From the Supervisor; Insufficient Non Monetary Recognition From the Supervisor; Non Monetary Recognition From the Supervisor Deficiency; Non Monetary Recognition From the Supervisor Deficit; Low Non Monetary Recognition From the Supervisor; Lack of Non Monetary Acknowledgment From the Supervisor; Lack of Non Monetary Appreciation From the Supervisor; Lack of Non Monetary Employee Recognition From the Supervisor.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LackOfRecognitionAndRewardSystem">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing an organizational situation characterized by the absence of structured and fair programs to recognize and reward contributions.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a company with no bonus policy or formal recognition practices.

Synonyms: Absence of Reward Structures; Inadequate Recognition System; Absence of Recognition and Reward System; Insufficient Recognition and Reward System; Recognition and Reward System Deficiency; Recognition and Reward System Deficit; Low Recognition and Reward System; Lack of Acknowledgment and Reward System; Lack of Appreciation and Reward System; Lack of Employee Recognition and Reward System.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LackOfTraining">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation characterized by the absence of structured onboarding, technical guidance, or role-based training.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a new hire expected to operate a system without any training.

Synonyms: Inadequate Training; Absence of Instruction; Absence of Training; Insufficient Training; Training Deficiency; Training Deficit; Low Training; Lack of Learning; Lack of Development; Lack of Instruction.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LackofLeaderMemberExchange">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a relational situation characterized by low-quality interaction, support, and trust between an employee and their supervisor.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who rarely receives information or support from their supervisor.

Synonyms: Lackof Leader Member Exchange; Low LMX Quality; Distant Supervisor Relationship; Lackof Leader Member Exchange factor; Lackof Leader Member Exchange condition; Lackof Leader Member Exchange antecedent; Lackof Leader Member Exchange driver; Lackof Leader Member Exchange indicator; Lackof Leader Member Exchange determinant; Absence of Leader Member Exchange; Insufficient Leader Member Exchange; Leader Member Exchange Deficiency.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Layoff">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Event type representing an organization-level termination of several employment relationships, usually driven by structural, economic, or strategic reasons.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it is the collective form of InvoluntaryTurnover and is disjoint from Dismissal.

Examples include a startup laying off 20% of its staff to extend its runway.

Synonyms: Also termed &quot;Downsizing&quot;, &quot;Retrenchment&quot;, or &quot;Reduction in Force (RIF)&quot; in human resource management.

References:

Abrams, L. R., Harknett, K., &amp; Schneider, D. (2022). Older workers with unpredictable schedules: Implications for well-being and job retention. The Gerontologist. https://doi.org/10.1093/geront/gnac067

Eib, C., Bernhard-Oettel, C., Leineweber, C., &amp; Näswall, K. (2021). You can’t always get what you want: Mechanisms and consequences of intra-organizational job change among middle managers in sweden. The International Journal of Human Resource Management. https://doi.org/10.1080/09585192.2021.1886153

Nazir, T., Umer, M., Najam, M., Nawab, S., Maqsoom, A., Shafi, K., Munir, Y., &amp; Nawaz, I. (2022). Impact role stress on turnover intentions of Pakistan’s healthcare workers: Mediating and moderating role of organizational cynicism and self-efficacy. PLoS ONE. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0279075</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LeaderExpressedHumility">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a leadership situation in which leaders acknowledge their limitations, value followers&apos; contributions, and model openness to learning.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a manager who openly asks the team for feedback on their own decisions.

Synonyms: leader humility; humble leadership; expressed leader humility; managerial humility; supervisor humility; humble leader behavior.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LearningOpportunities">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which employees have access to training, mentoring, challenging projects, and skill development.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee enrolled in a sponsored certification and assigned to a stretch project.

Synonyms: Skill Development Environment; Continuous Learning Opportunities in HR metrics; Learning Prospects; Learning Possibilities; Learning Chances.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LeaveDecisionMakingEvent">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Event type, specialization of DecisionMaking, in which an Employee deliberates and which may create a DecisionToLeave.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, the corresponding intention is manifested in it, and a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext may contribute to triggering it. Every endurant created in this event is a DecisionToLeave.

Examples include an employee weighing whether to accept a competitor&apos;s offer.

Synonyms: exit deliberation; leaving decision process; quit-decision process; resignation deliberation; turnover decision-making event; Leave Decision Making Event commitment; Leave Decision Making Event deliberative outcome; Leave Decision Making Event choice; Leave Decision Making Event intentional resolution; Leave Decision Making Event decision state.

References:

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf

Guizzardi, R., Perini, A., &amp; Susi, A. (2018). Aligning Goal and Decision Modeling. In Information Systems in the Big Data Era (pp. 124–132). https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-92901-9_12

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LeaveIntentionFormation">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Event type that classifies the events in which an IntentionToLeave comes into existence.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it is triggered by a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext (gufo:contributedToTrigger) and the IntentionToLeave was created in it (gufo:wasCreatedIn). The three formation event types are pairwise disjoint.

Examples include an employee who, after the arrival of a toxic manager, starts to consider resigning.

References:

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf

Mobley, W. H. (1977). Intermediate linkages in the relationship between job satisfaction and employee turnover. Journal of Applied Psychology, 62(2), 237–240.</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LessWorkingHoursSpentRemotely">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a work arrangement in which an employee has few or no options to work away from the employer&apos;s premises.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include an employee required to be in the office five days a week.

Synonyms: reduced remote-work hours; less telework; lower remote-work intensity; limited work from home; reduced telecommuting; greater office presence; Low Remote Work Allocation; Strict Office Presence Requirements.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LivingPerson">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Phase representing the active, biological lifetime status of a human being.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this is an anti-rigid phase of Person. An individual must be in this phase to assume the role of an Employee.

Examples include any active, living professional currently participating in the workforce.

Synonyms: Also termed &quot;Natural Person while Alive&quot; in foundational legal ontologies.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LongTermContracts">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a contractual situation characterized by open-ended or multi-year employment contracts.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee hired under an indefinite-term contract.

Synonyms: long-term employment contracts; permanent contracts; indefinite employment contracts; open-ended employment agreements; continuing employment contracts; secure employment contracts.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LoosenessCulture">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing an organizational culture characterized by weak social norms, high tolerance for deviant behavior, and lax rule enforcement.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a workplace where missed deadlines and rude behavior carry no consequences.

Synonyms: cultural looseness; permissive organizational culture; weak-norm culture; low-norm-enforcement culture; organizational looseness; flexible normative climate; Permissive Corporate Culture; Weak Normative Culture.

References:

Di Santo, D., Talamo, A., Bonaiuto, F., Cabras, C., &amp; Pierro, A. (2021). A multilevel analysis of the impact of unit Tightness vs. Looseness culture on attitudes and behaviors in the workplace. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.652068

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LossOfHighQualifiedAndSkilledEmployees">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which the organization loses highly qualified talent and specialized knowledge.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of voluntary or involuntary turnover for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerVoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext or an EmployerInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by the corresponding VoluntaryTurnover or InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a company that loses its only specialist in a critical system.

Synonyms: loss of key talent; skilled-employee loss; talent drain; human-capital loss; loss of high performers; expertise loss; Drain of Key Talent; Intellectual Capital Loss.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LossesToTheQualityDelivery">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which the quality or accuracy of delivered goods or services falls due to employee exits.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of voluntary or involuntary turnover for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerVoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext or an EmployerInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by the corresponding VoluntaryTurnover or InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include an increase in product defects after experienced operators leave.

Synonyms: delivery-quality loss; quality deterioration; decline in delivery quality; reduced output quality; service-delivery degradation; quality impairment; Quality Delivery Degradation.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LowBondingSocialCapital">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation characterized by weak internal relationships, low trust, and low cohesion within a work group.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a team whose members rarely interact beyond formal tasks.

Synonyms: Weak Team Cohesion; Low Social Capital; Reduced Bonding Social Capital; Limited Bonding Social Capital; Weak Bonding Social Capital; Bonding Social Capital Deficit; Low Level of Bonding Social Capital.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LowEmploymentStability">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing an employment situation in which the continuity of the employee&apos;s role is poorly guaranteed.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a worker on successive short-term contracts.

Synonyms: Contractual Instability; High Job Precarity; Reduced Employment Stability; Limited Employment Stability; Weak Employment Stability; Employment Stability Deficit; Low Level of Employment Stability; Low Employment Security; Low Employment Continuity; Low Employment Steadiness.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LowLevelsOfCompensationPlans">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a compensation situation in which the organization&apos;s pay scales and bonuses are significantly below market standards.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a company paying salaries 20% below the market median.

Synonyms: Below-Market Compensation; Low Pay Scale; Reduced Levels of Compensation Plans; Limited Levels of Compensation Plans; Weak Levels of Compensation Plans; Levels of Compensation Plans Deficit; Low Level of Levels of Compensation Plans.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LowLevelsOfDistributedLeadership">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a leadership situation characterized by highly centralized, top-down decision-making with little sharing of authority.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a department in which every decision must be approved by the director.

Synonyms: Centralized Leadership; Highly Hierarchical Management; Reduced Levels of Distributed Leadership; Limited Levels of Distributed Leadership; Weak Levels of Distributed Leadership; Levels of Distributed Leadership Deficit; Low Level of Levels of Distributed Leadership; Low Levels of Distributed Supervision; Low Levels of Distributed Management; Low Levels of Distributed Leader Behavior.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LowPerceivedExternalEmployability">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s perception that their chances of finding an equivalent or better job outside the current organization are low.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a specialist in a legacy system who believes few employers need their skills.

Synonyms: Low External Marketability; Low Perceived Job Alternatives; Reduced Perceived External Employability; Limited Perceived External Employability; Weak Perceived External Employability; Perceived External Employability Deficit; Low Level of Perceived External Employability; Low Subjective External Employability; Low Experienced External Employability; Low Perceived Level of External Employability.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LowPerformance">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">&quot;A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which an employee&apos;s work output, quality, or efficiency consistently falls below the standards expected by the organization.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential involuntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a InvoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as InvoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a TerminationIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or an involuntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who misses targets for three consecutive quarters.

Synonyms: Substandard Performance; Inadequate Work Output in performance management; Reduced Performance; Limited Performance; Weak Performance; Performance Deficit; Low Level of Performance; Low Effectiveness; Low Productivity; Low Work Performance.

References:

Di Stefano, G., Venza, G., &amp; Aiello, D. (2020). Associations of job insecurity with perceived work-related symptoms, job satisfaction, and turnover intentions: The mediating role of leader–member exchange and the moderating role of organizational support. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.01329

Farooq, H., Janjua, U. I., Madni, T. M., Waheed, A., Zareei, M., &amp; Alanazi, F. (2022). Identification and Analysis of Factors Influencing Turnover Intention of Pakistan IT Professionals: An Empirical Study. IEEE Access. https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2022.3181753

Hu, C., Zhang, S., Chen, Y., &amp; Griggs, T. L. (2022). A meta-analytic study of subjective career plateaus. Journal of Vocational Behavior. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jvb.2021.103649

Nazir, T., Umer, M., Najam, M., Nawab, S., Maqsoom, A., Shafi, K., Munir, Y., &amp; Nawaz, I. (2022). Impact role stress on turnover intentions of Pakistan’s healthcare workers: Mediating and moderating role of organizational cynicism and self-efficacy. PLoS ONE. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0279075

Nordgren Selar, A., Gagné, M., Hellgren, J., Falkenberg, H., &amp; Sverke, M. (2023). Compensation profiles among private sector employees in Sweden: Differences in work-related and health-related outcomes. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.949711

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf&quot;</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LowQualitySelectionProcess">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which recruitment was rushed or inaccurate, failing to align candidates&apos; expectations and skills with the role.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a candidate hired after a single short interview who discovers the job differs from the posting.

Synonyms: Poor Hiring Practices; Inadequate Screening Process; Reduced Quality Selection Process; Limited Quality Selection Process; Weak Quality Selection Process; Quality Selection Process Deficit; Low Level of Quality Selection Process.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LowQuantityOfWork">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which an employee&apos;s workload is manageable or under-allocated relative to their capacity.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee whose tasks comfortably fit within contracted hours.

Synonyms: Underemployment; Low Work Intensity in workload management; Reduced Quantity of Work; Limited Quantity of Work; Weak Quantity of Work; Quantity of Work Deficit; Low Level of Quantity of Work; Low Quantity of Job; Low Quantity of Employment; Low Quantity of Occupational.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LowerEconomicStabilityOfTheEmployer">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which the employing organization faces financial stress or reduced stability due to employee exits.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of voluntary or involuntary turnover for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerVoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext or an EmployerInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by the corresponding VoluntaryTurnover or InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a small firm whose cash flow is strained by replacement costs.

Synonyms: Diminished Corporate Financial Health; Reduced Economic Stability of the Employer; Decreased Economic Stability of the Employer; Diminished Economic Stability of the Employer; Lower Level of Economic Stability of the Employer; Lower Economic Stability of the Organization; Lower Economic Stability of the Company; Lower Economic Stability of the Firm; Lower Economic Security of the Employer; Lower Economic Continuity of the Employer; Lower Economic Steadiness of the Employer.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LowerGrowthOpportunities">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which a dismissed person faces fewer career paths or promotion opportunities.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of involuntary turnover for the employee. Its instances are elements of an EmployeeInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by an InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a dismissed professional who accepts a lower-level job.

Synonyms: Career Plateau; Lack of Advancement; Reduced Growth Opportunities; Decreased Growth Opportunities; Diminished Growth Opportunities; Lower Level of Growth Opportunities; Lower Growth Prospects; Lower Growth Possibilities; Lower Growth Chances; Lower Development Opportunities; Lower Advancement Opportunities; Lower Progression Opportunities.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LowerLearningOpportunities">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which a dismissed person has limited access to training and skill development.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of involuntary turnover for the employee. Its instances are elements of an EmployeeInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by an InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a dismissed worker without access to employer-sponsored training.

Synonyms: Skill Stagnation Environment; Lack of Professional Development; Reduced Learning Opportunities; Decreased Learning Opportunities; Diminished Learning Opportunities; Lower Level of Learning Opportunities; Lower Learning Prospects; Lower Learning Possibilities; Lower Learning Chances.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#LowerProfessionalSatisfaction">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which a dismissed person experiences low job fulfillment in subsequent work.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of involuntary turnover for the employee. Its instances are elements of an EmployeeInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by an InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a professional dissatisfied with the job accepted after dismissal.

Synonyms: Job Dissatisfaction State; Low Work Engagement; Reduced Professional Satisfaction; Decreased Professional Satisfaction; Diminished Professional Satisfaction; Lower Level of Professional Satisfaction; Lower Professional Contentment; Lower Professional Positive Appraisal.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

