    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#OpeningShifts">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a scheduling arrangement in which an employee is assigned to the first hours of an establishment&apos;s operation.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a bakery employee who starts at 5 a.m. to open the store.

Synonyms: opening-duty shift; early opening shift; start-of-day shift; morning opening shift; open shift; early-morning shift.

References:

Abrams, L. R., Harknett, K., &amp; Schneider, D. (2022). Older workers with unpredictable schedules: Implications for well-being and job retention. The Gerontologist. https://doi.org/10.1093/geront/gnac067

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#OptimisticConfidence">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s hopeful expectations and confidence in positive future career outcomes.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who is confident that their current path will lead to promotion.

Synonyms: positive expectancy; hopeful confidence; career optimism; generalized optimism; positive future orientation; optimistic self-assurance.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#OrganizationalTrust">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s willingness to be vulnerable to the organization&apos;s actions, based on the expectation that it will act fairly and in good faith.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who accepts a temporary pay freeze trusting management&apos;s explanation.

Synonyms: employee trust in the organization; workplace trust; institutional trust; trust in management; organizational confidence; Trust in the Employer; Corporate Trust; Company Trust.

References:

Stavrova, O., Spiridonova, T., Van De Calseyde, P. E., Meyers, C., &amp; Evans, A. M. (2023). Does remote work erode trust in organizations? A within-person investigation in the COVID-19 context. Social and Personality Psychology Compass. https://doi.org/10.1111/spc3.12762

Barattucci, M., Teresi, M., Pietroni, D., Iacobucci, S., Lo Presti, A., &amp; Pagliaro, S. (2021). Ethical climate(s), distributed leadership, and work outcomes: The mediating role of organizational identification. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.564112

Johannsen, R., &amp; Zak, P. J. (2021). The neuroscience of organizational trust and business performance: Findings from United States working adults and an intervention at an online retailer. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.579459

Georganta, K., &amp; Montgomery, A. (2022). Workplace fun is not enough: The role of work engagement and trust. Cogent Psychology. https://doi.org/10.1080/23311908.2022.2060603

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Overload">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which the workload, cognitive requirements, or physical demands placed on an employee exceed their capacity and standard hours.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include an employee responsible for three simultaneous projects with the same deadline.

Synonyms: work overload; role overload; job overload; excessive workload; work burden; demand overload; Work Burdening.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Ownership">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s psychological sense that their work, project, or organization is &quot;theirs&quot;.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an engineer who refers to the product they built as &quot;my system&quot;.

Synonyms: psychological ownership; sense of ownership; felt ownership; possessive attachment; territorial ownership; ownership feeling; Sense of Possession.

References:

Nazir, T., Umer, M., Najam, M., Nawab, S., Maqsoom, A., Shafi, K., Munir, Y., &amp; Nawaz, I. (2022). Impact role stress on turnover intentions of Pakistan’s healthcare workers: Mediating and moderating role of organizational cynicism and self-efficacy. PLoS ONE. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0279075

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#PerceptionOfAFulfillingJob">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s appraisal that their role offers meaningful and purposeful experiences.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a teacher who feels their work makes a real difference to students.

Synonyms: Meaningful Work Perception; Job Fulfillment Appraisal; Perception of A Fulfilling Work; Perception of A Fulfilling Role; Perception of A Fulfilling Position.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Person">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Phase (anti-rigid sortal) of Person representing a human being during their biological lifetime.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, a person must be in this phase to play the Employee or RetiredPerson roles; its age phases are Child, Adolescent, Adult, and Elderly.

Examples include any currently living employee.

Synonyms: Also termed &quot;Natural Person while Alive&quot; in foundational legal ontologies.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#PersonJobFit">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:ExtrinsicMode representing the congruence inhering in an employee between their knowledge, skills, and abilities and the demands of their job.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this extrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a detail-oriented analyst working in an auditing role.

Synonyms: individual-job fit; job fit; person-role fit; employee-job compatibility; abilities-demands fit; needs-supplies fit; PJ Fit; Skill-to-Task Alignment; Person Work Fit; Person Position Fit.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#PersonOrganizationFit">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:ExtrinsicMode representing the congruence inhering in an employee between their personal values and goals and the culture and values of the organization.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this extrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee committed to sustainability working for a company with strong environmental goals.

Synonyms: person–environment fit; individual-organization fit; employee-organization compatibility; value congruence; organizational fit; PO Fit.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#PhysicalAgent">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Category (rigid non-sortal) that classifies physical entities capable of acting as agents and of bearing subjective mental states grounded in individual experience.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, PhysicalAgent is the supertype of Person. Employee-side intentions (IntentionToLeave, IntentionToStay) and the psychological factors of turnover and retention inhere in physical agents playing the Employee role.

Examples include human individuals such as an employee, a manager, or a household employer.

Synonyms: Also termed &quot;physical entity&quot;, &quot;natural person&quot;, &quot;human agent&quot;, or &quot;individual agent&quot; in the foundational ontology and organizational literature.

References:

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Fonseca, C. M. (2026). gUFO: A gentle foundational ontology for Semantic Web knowledge graphs. Applied Ontology.

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Guizzardi, G. (2005). Ontological foundations for structural conceptual models (Doctoral dissertation). University of Twente.

Santana, A. C. (2024). OntoTurnover: Uma Abordagem Ontológica para Compreender a Rotatividade de Profissionais (Master&apos;s dissertation). Universidade de Pernambuco.</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Polychronicity">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s preference for engaging in multiple tasks or activities simultaneously.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an operations coordinator who enjoys handling several requests at once.

Synonyms: polychronic orientation; multitasking preference; preference for simultaneous activities; multiple-task time orientation; polychronic time use; concurrent-task preference.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#PositiveWorkRelatedExperiences">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation characterized by favorable and rewarding daily interactions and outcomes in the workplace.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee whose recent projects were successful and well received.

Synonyms: Favorable Workplace Incidents; Positive Work Events; Positive Job Related Experiences; Positive Employment Related Experiences; Positive Occupational Related Experiences.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ProfessionalOutdatedness">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which a dismissed person&apos;s skills or knowledge become obsolete relative to market demands.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of involuntary turnover for the employee. Its instances are elements of an EmployeeInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by an InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a professional unemployed for years whose technical skills fall behind.

Synonyms: skill obsolescence; professional obsolescence; occupational outdatedness; competence obsolescence; knowledge obsolescence; professional stagnation.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#PsychologicalCapital">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing a positive developmental state inhering in an employee characterized by hope, efficacy, resilience, and optimism.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who recovers quickly from setbacks and approaches challenges with confidence.

Synonyms: PsyCap; positive psychological capital; individual psychological capital; HERO resources; hope-efficacy-resilience-optimism capital; HERO State.

References:

Yan, Z., Mansor, Z. D., Choo, W. C., &amp; Abdullah, A. R. (2021). Mitigating effect of psychological capital on employees’ withdrawal behavior in the presence of job attitudes: Evidence from five-star hotels in Malaysia. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.617023

Ma, L., Zhou, F., &amp; Liu, H. (2021). Relationship between psychological empowerment and the retention intention of kindergarten teachers: A chain intermediary effect analysis. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.601992

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#PsychologicalSafety">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a team situation characterized by a shared belief that it is safe to take interpersonal risks, such as speaking up or admitting mistakes.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a team in which members openly report errors without fear of blame.

Synonyms: team psychological safety; interpersonal psychological safety; speak-up safety; interpersonal risk-taking safety; psychologically safe climate; Interpersonal Trust Climate; Psychological Security; Psychological Safety Climate.

References:

Al Kurdi, B., Alshurideh, M., &amp; Al Afaishat, T. (2020). Employee retention and organizational performance: Evidence from banking industry. Management Science Letters. https://doi.org/10.5267/j.msl.2020.7.011

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#PublicValue">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which the organization&apos;s mission and outputs generate recognizable benefits for society.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a sanitation company whose services visibly improve public health.

Synonyms: public-service value; societal value; value for the public; collective value; public benefit; social value; Societal Contribution; Public Good Generation.

References:

Grubert, T., Steuber, J., &amp; Meynhardt, T. (2022). Engagement at a higher level: The effects of public value on employee engagement, the organization, and society. Current Psychology: A Journal for Diverse Perspectives on Diverse Psychological Issues. https://doi.org/10.1007/s12144-022-03076-0

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#PureOrganicFun">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a social situation characterized by spontaneous and unforced enjoyable interactions among colleagues.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include colleagues who spontaneously organize lunch together and share jokes.

Synonyms: organic workplace fun; spontaneous workplace fun; informal workplace fun; naturally occurring fun at work; employee-generated fun; unstructured workplace enjoyment.

References:

Georganta, K., &amp; Montgomery, A. (2022). Workplace fun is not enough: The role of work engagement and trust. Cogent Psychology. https://doi.org/10.1080/23311908.2022.2060603

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#RecognitionAndRewardSystem">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which the organization has formal policies and mechanisms to recognize and reward employee contributions.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a company with a quarterly recognition program and performance-based bonuses.

Synonyms: Reward and Recognition Program; Performance Award Infrastructure; Acknowledgment and Reward System; Appreciation and Reward System; Employee Recognition and Reward System.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#RecruitmentCosts">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state of expenses incurred in sourcing, screening, and interviewing candidates to replace employees who left.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of voluntary or involuntary turnover for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerVoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext or an EmployerInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by the corresponding VoluntaryTurnover or InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include agency fees paid to replace a departed manager.

Synonyms: Sourcing Expenses; Candidate Acquisition Costs in recruiting metrics; Recruitment Expenses; Recruitment Expenditures; Recruitment Outlays.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ReductionOfCostsOfProductsAndServicesOffered">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which production or service delivery costs are reduced due to workforce stability.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of retention for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerRetentionConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by a Retention. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a plant whose unit costs fall as experienced staff remain.

Synonyms: COGS Reduction; Service Cost Optimization; Reduced Costs of Products and Services Offered; Decrease in Costs of Products and Services Offered; Decline in Costs of Products and Services Offered; Lower Costs of Products and Services Offered; Reduction of Expenses of Products and Services Offered; Reduction of Expenditures of Products and Services Offered; Reduction of Outlays of Products and Services Offered.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ReductionOfHiringCosts">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state of diminished administrative and contracting expenses due to lower attrition.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of retention for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerRetentionConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by a Retention. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a company that halves its annual hiring expenses after reducing turnover.

Synonyms: Hiring Cost Savings; Onboarding Savings; Reduced Hiring Costs; Decrease in Hiring Costs; Decline in Hiring Costs; Lower Hiring Costs; Reduction of Hiring Expenses; Reduction of Hiring Expenditures; Reduction of Hiring Outlays.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ReductionOfIntegrationCosts">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which the organization avoids adaptation-period costs by keeping existing staff.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of retention for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerRetentionConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by a Retention. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a team with no productivity loss from onboarding replacements.

Synonyms: Assimilation Cost Mitigation; Productivity Stability; Reduced Integration Costs; Decrease in Integration Costs; Decline in Integration Costs; Lower Integration Costs; Reduction of Integration Expenses; Reduction of Integration Expenditures; Reduction of Integration Outlays.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ReductionOfRecruitmentCosts">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state of reduced expenses on job postings, sourcing, and recruiter fees due to retention.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of retention for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerRetentionConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by a Retention. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a company that no longer needs external recruiters for most roles.

Synonyms: Recruiting Budget Savings; Sourcing Cost Reduction; Reduced Recruitment Costs; Decrease in Recruitment Costs; Decline in Recruitment Costs; Lower Recruitment Costs; Reduction of Recruitment Expenses; Reduction of Recruitment Expenditures; Reduction of Recruitment Outlays.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ReductionOfTrainingCosts">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state in which the organization avoids retraining and upskilling replacement employees.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of retention for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerRetentionConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by a Retention. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a company whose training budget for newcomers falls after attrition drops.

Synonyms: Retraining Expense Mitigation; Training Budget Optimization; Reduced Training Costs; Decrease in Training Costs; Decline in Training Costs; Lower Training Costs; Reduction of Training Expenses; Reduction of Training Expenditures; Reduction of Training Outlays; Reduction of Learning Costs; Reduction of Development Costs; Reduction of Instruction Costs.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Redundancy">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">&quot;A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which an employee&apos;s position is eliminated or made unnecessary by restructuring, exposing them to termination.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential involuntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a InvoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as InvoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a TerminationIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or an involuntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a role eliminated after the automation of a back-office process.

Synonyms: job redundancy; position elimination; role elimination; job abolition; workforce surplus; retrenchment.

References:

Nazir, T., Umer, M., Najam, M., Nawab, S., Maqsoom, A., Shafi, K., Munir, Y., &amp; Nawaz, I. (2022). Impact role stress on turnover intentions of Pakistan’s healthcare workers: Mediating and moderating role of organizational cynicism and self-efficacy. PLoS ONE. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0279075

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf&quot;</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#RegularIndividualMeetingsWithLeadership">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which recurring one-on-one meetings between an employee and their leader are established.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who has a 30-minute one-on-one with their manager every two weeks.

Synonyms: One-on-One Meetings; Supervisor Check-ins; Regular Individual Meetings with Supervision; Regular Individual Meetings with Management; Regular Individual Meetings with Leader Behavior.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#RepeatedJobCrafts">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which an employee repeatedly and proactively reshapes their task, cognitive, or relational job boundaries to fit their strengths.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who over several years reshapes their role toward data analysis.

Synonyms: Continuous Job Redesign; Proactive Role Adaptation; Repeated Work Crafts; Repeated Role Crafts; Repeated Position Crafts.

References:

Garthe, N., &amp; Hasselhorn, H. M. (2022). Changes of profession, employer and work tasks in later working life: An empirical overview of staying and leaving. Ageing &amp; Society. https://doi.org/10.1017/S0144686X21000088

Xin, X., Cai, W., Gao, X., &amp; Liu, T. (2021). Will job crafters stay or leave? The roles of organizational instrumentality and inclusive leadership. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.743828

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ReplacementDifficulty">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a post-state of significant delays and difficulty in finding suitable replacements for employees who left.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this post-state situation is a potential consequence of voluntary or involuntary turnover for the employer. Its instances are elements of an EmployerVoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext or an EmployerInvoluntaryTurnoverConsequenceContext through :isConsequenceElementOf (inverse :hasConsequenceElement), and that context is brought about (gufo:broughtAbout) by the corresponding VoluntaryTurnover or InvoluntaryTurnover. It is a contingent outcome whose presence, intensity, and duration vary across employees, employers, and employment settings.

Examples include a specialist position that remains vacant for eight months.

Synonyms: difficulty replacing employees; replacement challenge; hard-to-fill vacancy; replacement scarcity; talent replacement difficulty; successor shortage; Talent Scarcity Friction; Replacement Delays.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Retention">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Event type representing the action through which an employment relationship is deliberately continued, manifesting a DecisionToStay.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it brings about one employee-side and one employer-side retention consequence context and is disjoint from Turnover.

Examples include an employee who declines an external offer and renews their commitment to the company.

Synonyms: employee retention; staff retention; workforce retention; organizational retention; continued employment; employment continuation; employee continuance.

References:

Santana, A. C., Rodrigues, C. M. de O., Farias Junior, I. H. de, &amp; Santos, W. B. (2023). OntoTurnover: A lightweight domain ontology for modeling employee turnover. In 2023 18th Iberian Conference on Information Systems and Technologies (CISTI) (pp. 1–4). IEEE. https://doi.org/10.23919/CISTI58278.2023.10211799 - Santana, A. C., Farias Junior, I. H. de, Santos, W. B., &amp; Rodrigues, C. M. de O. (2023). OntoTurnover: Uma ontologia de domínio sobre a rotatividade de profissionais. In Anais Estendidos do XXII Simpósio Brasileiro de Qualidade de Software (pp. 13–18). Sociedade Brasileira de Computação. https://doi.org/10.5753/sbqsestendido.2023.235887

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#RetiredPerson">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Role (anti-rigid, relationally dependent sortal) played by a living person who holds a retirement status granted by an institution.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, the role is founded on the Retirement relator. It is not disjoint from Employee, since a retired person may be re-employed.

Examples include a former bank clerk receiving a pension, or a retiree who returns to work as a consultant.

Synonyms: retiree; pensioner; retired worker.

References:

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Retirement">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Relator (a relator kind) representing the formal bond that establishes a person&apos;s retired status and mediates the retired person and the institution that grants the retirement benefit.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, Retirement is the foundation of the RetiredPerson role. It is distinct from turnover: a person may retire from the workforce and still be re-employed later.

Examples include a retirement granted by a social security institution or by a private pension fund.

Synonyms: workforce retirement; labor-force exit; career exit; withdrawal from employment; cessation of working life.

References:

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Fonseca, C. M. (2026). gUFO: A gentle foundational ontology for Semantic Web knowledge graphs. Applied Ontology.

Garthe, N., &amp; Hasselhorn, H. M. (2022). Changes of profession, employer and work tasks in later working life: An empirical overview of staying and leaving. Ageing &amp; Society.

Santana, A. C. (2024). OntoTurnover: Uma Abordagem Ontológica para Compreender a Rotatividade de Profissionais (Master&apos;s dissertation). Universidade de Pernambuco.

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#RoleAmbiguity">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which an employee lacks clear information about their responsibilities, tasks, or performance criteria.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a new coordinator who does not know which decisions they are authorized to make.

Synonyms: role uncertainty; job-role ambiguity; unclear role expectations; role lack of clarity; role confusion; Unclear Job Expectations; Role Vagueness State.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#RoleBasedSkillTrainingForLeaders">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which supervisors and managers have completed structured training to develop leadership skills for their role.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a company whose new managers all complete a leadership development program.

Synonyms: Managerial Leadership Training; Supervisor Skill Development; Role Based Skill Learning For Leaders; Role Based Skill Development For Leaders; Role Based Skill Instruction For Leaders.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#RoleConflict">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which an employee faces incompatible demands or expectations from different sources within their role.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include an employee asked by two managers to prioritize conflicting deliverables.

Synonyms: incompatible role demands; job-role conflict; inter-role conflict; conflicting role expectations; role incompatibility; Work Role Conflict; Role Tension; Role Discord.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#SeasonedProfessional">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Phase (anti-rigid sortal) of LivingPerson representing the stage of a person&apos;s working life in which they have accumulated extensive experience, skills, and domain knowledge.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, a person in this phase is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. The person enters a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext directly through :isContextualFactorOf, since the three specialized factor properties apply only to situations, events, and aspects, and is therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an engineer with twenty years of experience in the sector.

Synonyms: experienced professional; veteran worker; senior professional; tenured expert; experienced employee; career veteran; Experienced Worker; Senior Professional Phase.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#SelfActualization">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s sense of fulfilling their potential and achieving personal growth and realization at work.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a researcher who feels their current work fully uses their abilities.

Synonyms: self-actualisation; self-realization; personal fulfillment; fulfillment of potential; personal growth fulfillment; need for self-fulfillment; Self-Realization State; Maslowian Fulfillment.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#SelfEfficacy">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s belief in their capability to successfully execute specific tasks or achieve specific goals.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an analyst confident in their ability to lead a new project.

Synonyms: perceived self-efficacy; personal efficacy; efficacy belief; belief in one&apos;s capabilities; competence belief; Task-Specific Confidence; Self-Efficacy Belief.

References:

Garthe, N., &amp; Hasselhorn, H. M. (2022). Changes of profession, employer and work tasks in later working life: An empirical overview of staying and leaving. Ageing &amp; Society. https://doi.org/10.1017/S0144686X21000088

Nazir, T., Umer, M., Najam, M., Nawab, S., Maqsoom, A., Shafi, K., Munir, Y., &amp; Nawaz, I. (2022). Impact role stress on turnover intentions of Pakistan’s healthcare workers: Mediating and moderating role of organizational cynicism and self-efficacy. PLoS ONE. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0279075

Heyns, M., &amp; Rothmann, S. (2021). Trust profiles: Associations with psychological need satisfaction, work engagement, and intention to leave. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.563542

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#SelfOrganizedTeams">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which a group of employees operates with high collective autonomy, sharing responsibilities and self-scheduling.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an agile team that plans and assigns its own work each sprint.

Synonyms: self-organizing teams; self-managing teams; autonomous work groups; self-directed teams; empowered teams; leaderless teams.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#SocialAgent">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Category (rigid non-sortal) that classifies social or institutional entities capable of acting as agents. Following UFO-C, social agents, like physical agents, can bear intentional moments (such as intentions, goals, and commitments), which are ascribed to the social agent itself rather than to any of its members. Social agents are distinguished from physical agents by the way these moments are constituted and exercised: they do not rest on individual subjective experience, but are established through collective or institutional acts (e.g., a board resolution or a management decision) and are carried out by physical agents who act on the social agent&apos;s behalf in delegated roles, such as representatives, managers, or directors. As a gUFO Category, it aggregates entities that carry distinct criteria of identity (such as juridical persons versus informal social collectives).

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, SocialAgent (including its normalized subclass InstitutionalAgent) provides the metaphysical foundation for corporate entities, organizations, and institutional employers. Employee-side intentions (such as IntentionToLeave or IntentionToStay) inhere in a PhysicalAgent (a Person playing the Employee role). The employer-side IntentionToTerminate inheres in the Employer itself; when the Employer is a juridical person, this intention belongs to the social agent, while its formation and execution are performed by the physical agents who represent it.

Examples include corporate entities, juridical organizations, and collectives.

Also termed &quot;social entity&quot;, &quot;institutional agent&quot;, &quot;non-physical agent&quot;, or &quot;collective social agent&quot; in the foundational ontology and organizational modeling literature.

References:

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Fonseca, C. M. (2026). gUFO: A gentle foundational ontology for Semantic Web knowledge graphs. Applied Ontology.

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Guizzardi, G. (2005). Ontological foundations for structural conceptual models (Doctoral dissertation). University of Twente.

Guizzardi, G., Falbo, R. A., &amp; Guizzardi, R. S. S. (2008). Grounding software domain ontologies in the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO): The case of the ODE software process ontology. In Proceedings of the XI Iberoamerican Workshop on Requirements Engineering and Software Environments (IDEAS 2008).

Santana, A. C. (2024). OntoTurnover: Uma Abordagem Ontológica para Compreender a Rotatividade de Profissionais (Master&apos;s dissertation). Universidade de Pernambuco.</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#StayDecisionMakingEvent">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Event type, specialization of DecisionMaking, in which an Employee deliberates and which may create a DecisionToStay.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, the corresponding intention is manifested in it, and a RetentionMotivatingContext may contribute to triggering it. Every endurant created in this event is a DecisionToStay.

Examples include an employee weighing a counteroffer from their current employer.

Synonyms: stay deliberation; retention decision process; continuance decision-making; remain-or-leave deliberation; staying decision event; Stay Decision Making Event commitment; Stay Decision Making Event deliberative outcome; Stay Decision Making Event choice; Stay Decision Making Event intentional resolution; Stay Decision Making Event decision state.

References:

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf

Guizzardi, R., Perini, A., &amp; Susi, A. (2018). Aligning Goal and Decision Modeling. In Information Systems in the Big Data Era (pp. 124–132). https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-92901-9_12

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#StayIntentionFormation">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Event type that classifies the events in which an IntentionToStay comes into existence.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it is triggered by a RetentionMotivatingContext (gufo:contributedToTrigger) and the IntentionToStay was created in it (gufo:wasCreatedIn). The three formation event types are pairwise disjoint.

Examples include an employee who, after joining a mentoring program, commits to building a career in the company.

References:

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf

Mobley, W. H. (1977). Intermediate linkages in the relationship between job satisfaction and employee turnover. Journal of Applied Psychology, 62(2), 237–240.</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#StockOptions">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a compensation situation in which employees hold contracts granting the right to purchase company shares at a predetermined price.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee holding options that vest over four years.

Synonyms: employee stock options; share options; equity options; option-based compensation; stock-option grants; employee share options; Equity Compensation; Share Option Grants.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#StrengthsUseForRelationships">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which an employee is encouraged and able to apply their personal strengths in relationships at work.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee known for empathy who is asked to mentor newcomers.

Synonyms: relational strengths use; interpersonal strengths utilization; strengths application in relationships; social strengths use; relationship-oriented strengths deployment; interpersonal strength enactment; Relational Strengths Utilization; Social Strengths Application.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#StrengthsUseForTasks">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which an employee is encouraged and able to apply their core strengths to their tasks.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a strong writer assigned to produce the team&apos;s key reports.

Synonyms: task-related strengths use; strengths utilization at work; task-based strengths application; job strengths use; signature-strength deployment; work-task strength enactment; Task-Based Strengths Utilization; Signature Strengths Application.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#SubjectiveAgeDiversity">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a work-group situation characterized by differences in how old team members feel relative to one another.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a team in which some members feel much younger or older than their peers.

Synonyms: perceived age diversity; subjective age heterogeneity; felt-age diversity; cognitive-age diversity; perceived age differences; subjective age dispersion; Subjective Age Disparity.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#SubjectiveHappiness">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s stable positive state of general happiness and life satisfaction.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who describes themselves as generally happy with life.

Synonyms: self-reported happiness; perceived happiness; personal happiness; subjective well-being; hedonic well-being; General Happiness; Affective Well-Being.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#TerminationDecisionMakingEvent">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Event type, specialization of DecisionMaking, in which an Employer deliberates and which may create a DecisionToTerminate.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, the corresponding intention is manifested in it, and a InvoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext may contribute to triggering it. Every endurant created in this event is a DecisionToTerminate.

Examples include a management committee evaluating whether to dismiss an employee.

Synonyms: termination deliberation; dismissal decision process; discharge decision-making; employment-ending deliberation; employer separation decision event; Termination Decision Making Event commitment; Termination Decision Making Event deliberative outcome; Termination Decision Making Event choice; Termination Decision Making Event intentional resolution; Termination Decision Making Event decision state.

References:

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf

Guizzardi, R., Perini, A., &amp; Susi, A. (2018). Aligning Goal and Decision Modeling. In Information Systems in the Big Data Era (pp. 124–132). https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-92901-9_12

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#TerminationIntentionFormation">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Event type that classifies the events in which an IntentionToTerminate comes into existence.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it is triggered by a InvoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext (gufo:contributedToTrigger) and the IntentionToTerminate was created in it (gufo:wasCreatedIn). The three formation event types are pairwise disjoint.

Examples include a company that, facing repeated low performance, begins to consider dismissing an employee.

References:

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). http://purl.org/nemo/doc/gufo

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf

Mobley, W. H. (1977). Intermediate linkages in the relationship between job satisfaction and employee turnover. Journal of Applied Psychology, 62(2), 237–240.</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ToxicLeadership">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a leadership situation characterized by abusive, bullying, or highly dysfunctional behavior by managers.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a manager who routinely humiliates team members.

Synonyms: destructive leadership; abusive leadership; harmful leadership; dysfunctional leadership; toxic supervision; Abusive Supervision; Toxic Management; Toxic Leader Behavior.

References:

Niinihuhta, M., &amp; Häggman-Laitila, A. (2022). A systematic review of the relationships between nurse leaders&apos; leadership styles and nurses&apos; work-related well-being. International Journal of Nursing Practice. https://doi.org/10.1111/ijn.13040

Labrague, L. J., Nwafor, C. E., &amp; Tsaras, K. (2020). Influence of toxic and transformational leadership practices on nurses&apos; job satisfaction, job stress, absenteeism and turnover intention: A cross-sectional study. Journal of Nursing Management. https://doi.org/10.1111/jonm.13053

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#ToxicityFreeWorkplacePolicy">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which the employer enforces clear anti-harassment, anti-bullying, and ethical conduct policies.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a company with a zero-tolerance harassment policy and an independent reporting channel.

Synonyms: Zero-Tolerance Harassment Policy; Anti-Bullying Workplace Policy; Toxicity Free Work Policy; Toxicity Free Job Policy; Toxicity Free Occupational Policy.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

