Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#TrainingAndDevelopmentPrograms">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which structured programs to enhance employees&apos; skills, knowledge, and capabilities are available.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a company offering an annual learning budget and internal academies.

Synonyms: Employee Training Initiatives; Professional Development Programs; Training and Development Initiatives; Training and Development Interventions; Training and Development Schemes; Learning and Development Programs; Development and Development Programs; Instruction and Development Programs.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#TransformationalLeadership">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a leadership situation in which leaders inspire, intellectually stimulate, and individually consider their followers.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a manager who shares a compelling vision and supports each member&apos;s development.

Synonyms: transformative leadership; inspirational leadership; visionary leadership; change-oriented leadership; charismatic-transformational leadership; Employee Training Initiatives; Professional Development Programs; Transformational Supervision; Transformational Management; Transformational Leader Behavior.

References:

Niinihuhta, M., &amp; Häggman-Laitila, A. (2022). A systematic review of the relationships between nurse leaders&apos; leadership styles and nurses&apos; work-related well-being. International Journal of Nursing Practice. https://doi.org/10.1111/ijn.13040

Labrague, L. J., Nwafor, C. E., &amp; Tsaras, K. (2020). Influence of toxic and transformational leadership practices on nurses&apos; job satisfaction, job stress, absenteeism and turnover intention: A cross-sectional study. Journal of Nursing Management. https://doi.org/10.1111/jonm.13053

Kipfelsberger, P., Raes, A., Herhausen, D., Kark, R., &amp; Bruch, H. (2022). Start with why: The transfer of work meaningfulness from leaders to followers and the role of dyadic tenure. Journal of Organizational Behavior. https://doi.org/10.1002/job.2649

Tian, H., Iqbal, S., Akhtar, S., Qalati, S. A., Anwar, F., &amp; Khan, M. A. S. (2020). The impact of transformational leadership on employee retention: Mediation and moderation through organizational citizenship behavior and communication. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.00314

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Turnover">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Event type representing the rupture of the employment relationship between an employee and an employer.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, every Turnover terminates an Employment (inverse of gufo:wasTerminatedIn). It is specialized into VoluntaryTurnover and InvoluntaryTurnover and is disjoint from Retention.

Examples include a resignation, a dismissal, or a collective layoff.

References:

Santana, A. C., Rodrigues, C. M. de O., Farias Junior, I. H. de, &amp; Santos, W. B. (2023). OntoTurnover: A lightweight domain ontology for modeling employee turnover. In 2023 18th Iberian Conference on Information Systems and Technologies (CISTI) (pp. 1–4). IEEE. https://doi.org/10.23919/CISTI58278.2023.10211799

Santana, A. C., Farias Junior, I. H. de, Santos, W. B., &amp; Rodrigues, C. M. de O. (2023). OntoTurnover: Uma ontologia de domínio sobre a rotatividade de profissionais. In Anais Estendidos do XXII Simpósio Brasileiro de Qualidade de Software (pp. 13–18). Sociedade Brasileira de Computação. https://doi.org/10.5753/sbqsestendido.2023.235887</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#TurnoverContext">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a pre-state relevant to turnover or retention, which aggregates concrete contextual factors.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, factors of any nature enter the context through subproperties of :isContextualFactorOf (situations, events, and aspects), and the context, not the factor, contributes to triggering an IntentionFormation or a DecisionMaking event. It is specialized into TurnoverRiskContext and RetentionMotivatingContext.

Examples include the situation of an employee combining overload, a toxic manager, and burnout.

Synonyms: turnover situation; employee-exit context; separation context; turnover circumstances; employment-exit state of affairs.

References:

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, G., Benevides, A. B., Fonseca, C. M., Porello, D., Almeida, J. P. A., &amp; Sales, T. P. (2022). UFO: Unified Foundational Ontology. Applied Ontology, 17(1), 167–210. https://doi.org/10.3233/AO-210256

Santana, A. C., Rodrigues, C. M. de O., Farias Junior, I. H. de, &amp; Santos, W. B. (2023). OntoTurnover: A lightweight domain ontology for modeling employee turnover. In 2023 18th Iberian Conference on Information Systems and Technologies (CISTI) (pp. 1–4). IEEE. https://doi.org/10.23919/CISTI58278.2023.10211799

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Master&apos;s thesis, Lund University]. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#TurnoverRiskContext">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a pre-state that may contribute to triggering an employee&apos;s intention to leave or an employer&apos;s intention to terminate.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it is specialized into VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext and InvoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext and is disjoint from the retention contexts.

Examples include a period of restructuring combined with pay cuts.

Synonyms: turnover-risk situation; exit-risk context; separation-risk circumstances; attrition-risk setting; turnover-risk configuration.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#UnpredictableSchedules">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a scheduling arrangement in which an employee&apos;s working hours change frequently and without notice.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a retail worker who receives the weekly schedule only one day in advance.

Synonyms: schedule unpredictability; unstable work schedules; variable work schedules; volatile work hours; irregular scheduling; just-in-time scheduling.

References:

Abrams, L. R., Harknett, K., &amp; Schneider, D. (2022). Older workers with unpredictable schedules: Implications for well-being and job retention. The Gerontologist. https://doi.org/10.1093/geront/gnac067

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#UnsafeWorkingConditions">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a workplace situation characterized by high physical safety risks, inadequate ergonomics, or poor occupational health standards.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a warehouse without adequate protective equipment.

Synonyms: hazardous working conditions; unsafe workplace; occupational hazards; dangerous work environment; poor occupational safety; adverse safety conditions.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#VariablePay">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a compensation situation in which part of the employee&apos;s pay varies according to individual, team, or company performance.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential employer-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployerInfluencedRetentionContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include a salesperson whose bonus depends on quarterly targets.

Synonyms: variable compensation; incentive pay; performance-related pay; contingent pay; at-risk compensation; pay for performance; Performance-Linked Pay; Incentive Compensation.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#Vitality">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s positive state of physical energy, enthusiasm, and mental alertness.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who feels energized throughout the working day.

Synonyms: vigor; energy; aliveness; subjective vitality; work-related vitality; energetic activation; Energy State.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#VoluntaryTurnover">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Event type representing turnover initiated by the employee&apos;s own choice.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, it manifests a DecisionToLeave and brings about one employee-side and one employer-side consequence context (two separate existential restrictions, never their intersection). It is disjoint from InvoluntaryTurnover.

Examples include an analyst resigning to join a competitor.

Synonyms: voluntary employee turnover; voluntary separation; employee-initiated separation; voluntary exit; resignation; quitting; avoidable turnover.

References:

