Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#WorkFamilyConflict">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which the demands of an employee&apos;s work role make it difficult to meet family responsibilities.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include an employee whose shift schedule prevents them from picking up their children.

Synonyms: work-to-family conflict; work-home interference; work–life conflict; interrole work-family conflict; Interrole Conflict; Job Family Conflict; Employment Family Conflict; Occupational Family Conflict; Work Family Incompatibility; Work Family Tension; Work Family Discord.

References:

Abrams, L. R., Harknett, K., &amp; Schneider, D. (2022). Older workers with unpredictable schedules: Implications for well-being and job retention. The Gerontologist. https://doi.org/10.1093/geront/gnac067

Asakura, K., Asakura, T., Satoh, M., Watanabe, I., &amp; Hara, Y. (2020). Health indicators as moderators of occupational commitment and nurses&apos; intention to leave. Japan Journal of Nursing Science. https://doi.org/10.1111/jjns.12277

Garthe, N., &amp; Hasselhorn, H. M. (2022). Changes of profession, employer and work tasks in later working life: An empirical overview of staying and leaving. Ageing &amp; Society. https://doi.org/10.1017/S0144686X21000088

Heras, M. L., Rofcanin, Y., Escribano, P. I., Kim, S., &amp; Mayer, M. C. J. (2021). Family‐supportive organisational culture, work–family balance satisfaction and government effectiveness: Evidence from four countries. Human Resource Management Journal. https://doi.org/10.1111/1748-8583.12317

Nazir, T., Umer, M., Najam, M., Nawab, S., Maqsoom, A., Shafi, K., Munir, Y., &amp; Nawaz, I. (2022). Impact role stress on turnover intentions of Pakistan’s healthcare workers: Mediating and moderating role of organizational cynicism and self-efficacy. PLoS ONE. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0279075

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#WorkForLargeCompaniesWithoutPrestige">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a situation in which an employee works for a large organization lacking positive reputation or social prestige.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include an employee of a large firm with a poor public image.

Synonyms: Working in Unprestigious Large Companies; Low Corporate Pride State; Job For Large Companies Without Prestige; Employment For Large Companies Without Prestige; Occupational For Large Companies Without Prestige.

References:

Enander, A., &amp; Cardoso, J. (2020). How is employee turnover related to employee retention? A systematic review on two sets of meta-analyses [Dissertação de mestrado, Lund University]. Lund University Publications. http://lup.lub.lu.se/student-papers/record/9022943/file/9022946.pdf

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#WorkLifeBalanceSatisfaction">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:IntrinsicMode representing an employee&apos;s satisfaction with the balance between work demands and personal and family life.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this intrinsic mode inheres in an Employee (gufo:inheresIn) and is a potential employee-influenced retention factor. Its instances enter a EmployeeInfluencedRetentionContext through :isAspectualFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:standsIn and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as RetentionPotentialFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a StayIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a retention actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who can attend family commitments without harming their work.

Synonyms: Work-Family Balance Satisfaction; Work-Life Integration Contentment Situation; Job-Life Balance Satisfaction; Employment-Life Balance Satisfaction; Occupational-Life Balance Satisfaction; Work-Life Balance Contentment; Work-Life Balance Positive Appraisal.

References:

Heras, M. L., Rofcanin, Y., Escribano, P. I., Kim, S., &amp; Mayer, M. C. J. (2021). Family‐supportive organisational culture, work–family balance satisfaction and government effectiveness: Evidence from four countries. Human Resource Management Journal. https://doi.org/10.1111/1748-8583.12317

Asakura, K., Asakura, T., Satoh, M., Watanabe, I., &amp; Hara, Y. (2020). Health indicators as moderators of occupational commitment and nurses&apos; intention to leave. Japan Journal of Nursing Science. https://doi.org/10.1111/jjns.12277

Nazir, T., Umer, M., Najam, M., Nawab, S., Maqsoom, A., Shafi, K., Munir, Y., &amp; Nawaz, I. (2022). Impact role stress on turnover intentions of Pakistan’s healthcare workers: Mediating and moderating role of organizational cynicism and self-efficacy. PLoS ONE. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0279075

Abrams, L. R., Harknett, K., &amp; Schneider, D. (2022). Older workers with unpredictable schedules: Implications for well-being and job retention. The Gerontologist. https://doi.org/10.1093/geront/gnac067

Kopperud, K. H., Nerstad, C. G. L., &amp; Dysvik, A. (2020). Should I stay or should I go? The role of motivational climate and work–home spillover for turnover intentions. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.01107

Hai, S., &amp; Park, I. (2022). Strengths use for tasks and relationships in organizations: Development and validation of a Strengths Use Scale. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2022.659046

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#WorkplaceIncivility">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a social situation characterized by low-intensity rude or disrespectful behavior with ambiguous intent to harm.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include colleagues who routinely ignore greetings and interrupt others.

Synonyms: workplace rudeness; workplace discourtesy; low-intensity workplace mistreatment; uncivil workplace behavior; interpersonal disrespect at work; Rudeness at Work; Low Interpersonal Respect; Work Incivility; Job Incivility; Occupational Incivility.

References:

Tricahyadinata, I., Hendryadi, S., Zainurossalamia, SA., &amp; Riadi, S. S. (2020). Workplace incivility, work engagement, and turnover intentions: Multi-group analysis. Cogent Psychology. https://doi.org/10.1080/23311908.2020.1743627

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#WorkplaceOstracism">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a social situation in which an employee is systematically ignored or excluded by colleagues or managers.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include an employee who is left out of team meetings and informal gatherings.

Synonyms: workplace exclusion; social exclusion at work; employee ostracism; workplace isolation; coworker exclusion; Work Ostracism; Job Ostracism; Occupational Ostracism.

References:

Zhu, Y., &amp; Zhang, D. (2021). Workplace ostracism and counterproductive work behaviors: The chain mediating role of anger and turnover intention. Frontiers in Psychology. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.761560

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#12x36Shifts">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a shift-work arrangement in which an employee works 12 consecutive hours followed by 36 hours of rest.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a nurse on a hospital ward working 7 a.m. to 7 p.m. every other day.

Synonyms: 12-on/36-off schedule; twelve-hour shift followed by 36 hours off; 12-hour rotating shift; 12-by-36 shift pattern; 12/36 work schedule.

References:

Asakura, K., Asakura, T., Satoh, M., Watanabe, I., &amp; Hara, Y. (2020). Health indicators as moderators of occupational commitment and nurses&apos; intention to leave. Japan Journal of Nursing Science. https://doi.org/10.1111/jjns.12277

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://purl.org/ontoturnover#24x48Shifts">
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A gufo:Situation representing a shift-work arrangement in which an employee works a continuous 24-hour shift followed by 48 hours of rest.

In the context of the OntoTurnover domain, this situation is a potential voluntary turnover risk factor. Its instances enter a VoluntaryTurnoverRiskContext through :isSituationalFactorOf, a subproperty of gufo:isSituationProperPartOf and :isContextualFactorOf, and are therefore classified by the reasoner as VoluntaryTurnoverPotentialRiskFactor. The context, not the factor itself, may contribute to triggering a LeaveIntentionFormation (gufo:contributedToTrigger), and the property chain :contributedToFormationOf infers the resulting intention when one is recorded. Being a factor does not entail that an intention, a decision, or a voluntary turnover actually occurs.

Examples include a firefighter or security guard working one full day and resting the next two.

Synonyms: 24-on/48-off schedule; twenty-four-hour shift followed by 48 hours off; 24-hour rotating shift; 24-by-48 shift pattern; 24/48 work schedule.

References:

Asakura, K., Asakura, T., Satoh, M., Watanabe, I., &amp; Hara, Y. (2020). Health indicators as moderators of occupational commitment and nurses&apos; intention to leave. Japan Journal of Nursing Science. https://doi.org/10.1111/jjns.12277

Almeida, J. P. A., Guizzardi, G., Sales, T. P., &amp; Falbo, R. A. (2019). gUFO: A Lightweight Implementation of the Unified Foundational Ontology (UFO). https://nemo-ufes.github.io/gufo/

Guizzardi, R., Carneiro, B. G., Porello, D., &amp; Guizzardi, G. (2020). A core ontology on decision making. CEUR Workshop Proceedings, 2728, 9–21. https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-2728/paper1.pdf</rdfs:comment>
    </rdf:Description>
    


